(1914, 'What is Copy Elision, Return Value Optimization (RVO), and Named RVO (NRVO) in C++17?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nConstructs the returned object directly in the caller\'s storage; C++17 makes prvalue copy elision mandatory by language standard.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1915, 'What are Fold Expressions in C++17 variadic templates?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nAllows unpacking variadic template parameter packs over binary operators like (... + args) or (... && predicates) with concise syntax.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1916, 'How does std::is_invocable and std::invoke work across member function pointers, free functions, and lambdas?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nstd::invoke provides a unified calling mechanism regardless of whether the callable is a lambda, functor, free function, or member pointer.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1909, 'What is False Sharing in multithreaded C++ programs and how does alignas(hardware_destructive_interference_size) prevent it?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nOccurs when independent variables reside on the same CPU cache line (64 bytes), causing cache invalidation ping-pong. alignas pads variables to separate cache lines.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1910, 'How does std::string_view provide zero-copy string slicing and what are the lifetime dangling pitfalls?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nStores pointer and length without allocating heap memory; dangling occurs if the underlying owner string is destroyed before the view.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1911, 'What is std::span in C++20 and how does it replace raw buffer pointer-length function arguments?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nA non-owning view over contiguous sequences (arrays, vectors) providing bounds safety and uniform APIs.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1912, 'How does std::variant implement type-safe discriminated unions and how do you use std::visit with the overloaded pattern?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nstd::variant holds one of several types safely without heap allocations. std::visit dispatches pattern matching using custom lambda overloads.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1913, 'How does std::optional avoid sentinel values and represent nullable data cleanly in modern C++?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nHolds a value or std::nullopt without dynamic allocation, with methods like value_or(), and monadic operations (and_then, transform in C++23).\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1907, 'How does std::jthread in C++20 improve thread lifecycle management over std::thread?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nstd::jthread automatically joins upon destruction and supports cooperative cancellation tokens via std::stop_token.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1908, 'Explain C++ Memory Orders (memory_order_relaxed, memory_order_acquire, memory_order_release, memory_order_seq_cst) in std::atomic.', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nControls instruction reordering across threads. Relaxed guarantees atomicity only; Acquire-Release synchronizes read/write boundaries; Seq_Cst enforces global sequential consistency.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1904, 'How does std::expected in C++23 provide monadic error handling without exceptions?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nHolds either an expected value or an unexpected error (std::unexpected), supporting and_then(), transform(), and or_else() pipelines.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1905, 'What is SIMD vectorization and how do compiler intrinsics and std::experimental::simd accelerate computational throughput in C++?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nProcesses multiple data elements in a single CPU instruction (AVX2, AVX-512), dramatically improving numeric algorithm throughput.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1906, 'What are the differences between constexpr, consteval, and constinit in C++20?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nconstexpr evaluates at compile or runtime; consteval forces compile-time execution (immediate functions); constinit guarantees compile-time variable initialization without dynamic initialization order fiasco.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1897, 'What is the Empty Base Optimization (EBO) and how does [[no_unique_address]] in C++20 replace it?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nEBO allows 0-byte base classes to consume 0 bytes in derived classes. [[no_unique_address]] allows empty member variables to take 0 bytes without inheritance.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1898, 'How does std::shared_mutex enable reader-writer locking in C++17?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nAllows multiple concurrent shared readers (shared_lock) while guaranteeing exclusive access for writers (unique_lock).\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1899, 'What is the Strict Aliasing Rule in C++ and how does std::bit_cast in C++20 safely reinterpret binary types?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nCompiler assumes pointers of different types never point to the same memory. std::bit_cast copies bits safely without undefined behavior.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1900, 'What are Structured Bindings in C++17 and how do you customize tuple_size and get for custom structs?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nauto [a, b] = my_pair; unpacks tuples, arrays, and structs. Custom types implement std::tuple_size and get<I>() to support structured binding.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1901, 'How does type erasure work in std::function vs custom lightweight type-erased callable wrappers?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nstd::function uses virtual calls and potential heap allocations; custom type erasers use function pointer vtables for zero-overhead dispatch.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1902, 'What is the CRTP (Curiously Recurring Template Pattern) and how does it achieve static polymorphism in C++?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nClass Derived : public Base<Derived> resolves polymorphic method calls at compile time without vtable pointer overhead.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1903, 'How does std::format in C++20 replace printf and std::stringstream with type-safe high-speed formatting?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nProvides Python-like formatting syntax ({:02x}), compile-time format string validation, and superior performance compared to iostreams.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
