(1626, 'What are the 5 core SQL sub-languages (DDL, DML, DQL, DCL, TCL) and their functions?', '### SQL Sub-Languages\n\n- DDL (CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE): Schema definition.\n- DML (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE): Data manipulation.\n- DQL (SELECT): Querying.\n- DCL (GRANT, REVOKE): Permissions.\n- TCL (COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT): Transactions.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1627, 'What are the execution differences between TRUNCATE, DELETE, and DROP in SQL?', '### TRUNCATE vs DELETE vs DROP\n\n- DELETE (DML): Row-by-row, triggers fire, rollback supported.\n- TRUNCATE (DDL): Page deallocation, fast, resets auto-increment.\n- DROP (DDL): Completely removes table structure and data.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1628, 'Explain INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL OUTER, CROSS, and SELF JOINs in SQL.', '### SQL JOIN Types\n\n- INNER: Matching rows in both tables.\n- LEFT: All left rows + matching right rows.\n- RIGHT: All right rows + matching left rows.\n- FULL OUTER: All rows from both tables.\n- CROSS: Cartesian product.\n- SELF: Joining a table to itself for hierarchies.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1629, 'What is the exact logical execution order of an SQL query from FROM to LIMIT?', '### SQL Logical Processing Order\n\n1. FROM / JOIN\n2. WHERE\n3. GROUP BY\n4. HAVING\n5. SELECT\n6. DISTINCT\n7. ORDER BY\n8. LIMIT / OFFSET', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1630, 'How does Three-Valued Logic (3VL) work with NULLs in SQL?', '### Three-Valued Logic in SQL\n\nComparisons with NULL yield UNKNOWN. `col = NULL` is never true; use `IS NULL` and `COALESCE(col, default)`.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1631, 'How do Window Functions (OVER, PARTITION BY, ORDER BY) differ from GROUP BY in SQL?', '### Window Functions vs GROUP BY\n\nGROUP BY collapses rows into summary rows. Window functions compute running totals and rankings across partitions without collapsing rows.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1632, 'What are the differences between ROW_NUMBER(), RANK(), and DENSE_RANK()?', '### ROW_NUMBER vs RANK vs DENSE_RANK\n\n- ROW_NUMBER: 1, 2, 3, 4 (No ties).\n- RANK: 1, 1, 3, 4 (Ties leave gaps).\n- DENSE_RANK: 1, 1, 2, 3 (Ties leave no gaps).', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1633, 'How do Recursive CTEs traverse hierarchical trees and organizational charts in SQL?', '### Recursive CTEs\n\nUses anchor query (roots) UNION ALL recursive query (children) joined to CTE to traverse parent-child hierarchies.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1634, 'Explain Database Normalization from 1NF to BCNF and when to denormalize.', '### Normalization\n\n- 1NF: Atomic values + PK.\n- 2NF: No partial key dependencies.\n- 3NF: No transitive dependencies.\n- BCNF: Every determinant is a candidate key.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1635, 'What are B+Tree Clustered vs Non-Clustered Indexes in relational databases?', '### Clustered vs Non-Clustered Indexes\n\n- Clustered (PK): Leaf nodes contain actual physical row data.\n- Non-Clustered (Secondary): Leaf nodes contain index keys and PK pointers.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1636, 'What is a Correlated Subquery and why is an INNER JOIN often faster?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nCorrelated subqueries execute once per outer row; JOINs allow database optimizers to use hash or merge join algorithms.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1637, 'What is the difference between UNION and UNION ALL in SQL?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nUNION performs deduplication sort; UNION ALL concatenates directly without sorting, making it faster.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1638, 'How do Semi-Joins (EXISTS) and Anti-Joins (NOT EXISTS) operate in SQL?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nSemi-join checks existence of matches; Anti-join filters for absence of matches.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1639, 'How do LAG() and LEAD() calculate period-over-period differences in SQL?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nAccess preceding and succeeding rows within a partition without self-joins.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1640, 'What is a Covering Index and how does it prevent disk I/O bookmark lookups?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nAn index containing all SELECT and WHERE columns, answering queries directly from the index tree.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1641, 'What are the 4 ANSI Transaction Isolation Levels and what anomalies do they prevent?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nRead Uncommitted, Read Committed, Repeatable Read, Serializable prevent dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1642, 'What is MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control) in relational databases?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nMaintains row version snapshots in undo logs so readers don\'t block writers and writers don\'t block readers.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1643, 'What are Gap Locks and Next-Key Locks in InnoDB?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nGap locks lock index ranges between records to prevent phantom inserts; Next-Key locks combine record and gap locks.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1644, 'What is Keyset Pagination and why is it superior to OFFSET/LIMIT?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nUses indexed WHERE id < last_id ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20 for O(log N) index seeks on large datasets.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1645, 'What are the trade-offs between Auto-Increment integers and UUID v7 for Primary Keys?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nAuto-increment causes sequential clustering; UUID v7 provides time-ordered global uniqueness without B-Tree page splits.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1646, 'How do Check Constraints (CHECK) enforce data integrity at the database layer?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nEnsures columns satisfy business rules (e.g. price > 0, age >= 18) before commits.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1647, 'What is the difference between Views and Materialized Views in SQL?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nViews execute dynamically on read; Materialized Views persist query results on disk and refresh periodically.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1648, 'How does Table Partitioning (Range, List, Hash) differ from Database Sharding?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nPartitioning divides tables across disks in one instance; Sharding distributes data across multiple independent servers.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1649, 'How do Stored Procedures differ from User-Defined Functions (UDF) in SQL?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nProcedures execute transactions and return result sets; Functions return scalar values and can be used inside SELECT.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
(1650, 'How do Database Triggers work and why should business logic in triggers be limited?', '### SQL Query Engineering\n\nAutomate actions on DML events; complex triggers obscure business rules and hinder debugging.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAnalyze queries using `EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE` and ensure proper index coverage.', 'SQL Query Mastery & Database Design'),
