(1778, 'How do you map custom date and datetime formats using $casts in Eloquent?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\n\'created_at\' => \'datetime:Y-m-d H:i:s\', \'published_date\' => \'immutable_date:Y-m-d\'.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1777, 'How to configure full-text search indexes in Laravel migrations and query with whereFullText?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\n$table->fullText([\'title\', \'body\']); and Post::whereFullText([\'title\', \'body\'], \'database indexing\')->get();\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1776, 'How to handle Soft Delete unique constraint conflicts in MySQL with Laravel?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nUse composite unique index with deleted_at or MySQL 8 generated columns.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1774, 'How do you define and use Local Scopes with optional parameters in Eloquent?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\npublic function scopeOfType(Builder $query, string $type = \'standard\'): void { $query->where(\'type\', $type); }\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1775, 'How does firstOrFail() vs firstOr() vs sole() operate in Eloquent?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nfirstOrFail throws 404/ModelNotFoundException; firstOr executes closure fallback; sole guarantees exactly 1 record matches or throws exception.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1773, 'How does $touches cascade parent updated_at timestamp updates across child models?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nSetting protected $touches = [\'post\']; on Comment updates the parent post timestamp whenever comment is updated or deleted.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1772, 'How to perform atomic column increments and decrements in Laravel with extra updates?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nUser::where(\'id\', $id)->increment(\'credits\', 50, [\'last_awarded_at\' => now()]);\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1771, 'How does Schema::dump() and schema squashing accelerate database test suites in Laravel?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nDumps full SQL schema into a single schema file, bypassing sequential migrations execution in test environments.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1770, 'How to switch database connections per tenant in multi-tenant Laravel systems?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nSet connection at runtime: $model->setConnection(\'tenant_2\'); or DB::purge(\'tenant\'); Config::set(\'database.connections.tenant.database\', $dbName);\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1769, 'How do you force Eloquent to read from the Write (Master) connection for fresh queries?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nUse Model::onWriteConnection()->find($id); or DB::connection()->useWriteConnection();\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1768, 'How to configure and read from Read/Write MySQL connection pools in config/database.php?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nConfigure \'read\' => [\'host\' => [\'10.0.0.1\', \'10.0.0.2\']] and \'write\' => [\'host\' => \'10.0.0.3\'] under mysql driver.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1765, 'How do you perform Subquery Selects using addSelect() in Eloquent?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nUse $query->addSelect([\'last_login\' => Login::select(\'created_at\')->whereColumn(\'user_id\', \'users.id\')->latest()->limit(1)]);\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1766, 'How do you perform Subquery Ordering in Laravel?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nUse ->orderByDesc(Order::select(\'created_at\')->whereColumn(\'user_id\', \'users.id\')->latest()->limit(1)).\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1767, 'How does tap() and when() conditional clauses clean up complex dynamic query builder chains?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\n->when($request->status, fn($q, $s) => $q->where(\'status\', $s))->when($request->search, fn($q, term) => $q->where(\'title\', \'like\', \"%$term%\")).\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1764, 'How does whereRaw() and selectRaw() safely bind parameters to prevent SQL injection in Laravel?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nPass parameter array as 2nd argument: ->whereRaw(\'price > ? AND status = ?\', [100, \'active\']).\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1763, 'How do you store and query spatial geographic coordinates in Laravel?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nUse $table->geometry(\'location\') and query with whereRaw(\'ST_Distance_Sphere(location, POINT(?, ?)) < ?\').\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1762, 'How to create composite unique indexes and composite primary keys in Laravel migrations?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nUse $table->unique([\'user_id\', \'role_id\']); or $table->primary([\'order_id\', \'product_id\']);\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1761, 'How do you configure foreignId constraints with cascade on delete in migrations?', '### Laravel Database & Eloquent Engineering\n\nUse $table->foreignId(\'user_id\')->constrained()->cascadeOnDelete()->cascadeOnUpdate();\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAlways verify query efficiency with `DB::listen()` and enforce strict model modes during development.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1760, 'What are the differences between updateOrInsert(), firstOrCreate(), and updateOrCreate()?', '### updateOrInsert vs firstOrCreate vs updateOrCreate\n\n- `updateOrInsert(attributes, values)`: Query builder method. Executes raw SQL without hydrating Eloquent models or firing events.\n- `firstOrCreate(attributes, values)`: Returns existing Eloquent model or creates and returns new instance.\n- `updateOrCreate(attributes, values)`: Finds matching model or creates, updates attributes, and returns hydrated model.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1759, 'How does upsert() work in Laravel Query Builder and how does it translate to MySQL?', '### Eloquent & Query Builder upsert()\n\n```php\nDB::table(\'flight_targets\')->upsert([\n    [\'departure\' => \'OAK\', \'destination\' => \'SAN\', \'price\' => 99],\n    [\'departure\' => \'OAK\', \'destination\' => \'PDX\', \'price\' => 120]\n], [\'departure\', \'destination\'], [\'price\']);\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1758, 'When and why should you use saveQuietly(), updateQuietly(), and deleteQuietly()?', '### saveQuietly()\n\nExecutes the database update without triggering model events or observers. Ideal for internal audits, background counters, or sync jobs to avoid infinite event loops.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1756, 'How do you create, apply, and bypass an Anonymous or Class-based Global Scope in Laravel?', '### Global Scopes\n\n```php\n// 1. Anonymous Global Scope on Model:\nprotected static function booted(): void {\n    static::addGlobalScope(\'active\', fn($builder) => $builder->where(\'is_active\', true));\n}\n\n// 2. Bypassing Global Scope in queries:\n$allUsers = User::withoutGlobalScope(\'active\')->get();\n$allUsers = User::withoutGlobalScopes()->get();\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1757, 'What is the lifecycle of Eloquent Model Events and how do you register a Model Observer?', '### Model Events & Observers\n\nLifecycle: `retrieved` -> `creating` -> `created` -> `updating` -> `updated` -> `saving` -> `saved` -> `deleting` -> `deleted`.\n\n```php\nclass UserObserver {\n    public function creating(User $user): void {\n        $user->uuid = (string) Str::uuid();\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1755, 'How do you cast PHP 8.1 String-Backed Enums in Eloquent models?', '### Enum Casting in Eloquent\n\n```php\nenum PaymentStatus: string {\n    case Pending = \'pending\';\n    case Paid = \'paid\';\n    case Failed = \'failed\';\n}\n\nclass Invoice extends Model {\n    protected function casts(): array {\n        return [\n            \'status\' => PaymentStatus::class,\n        ];\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1754, 'How do you create a custom reusable Eloquent Cast class in Laravel?', '### Custom Casts\n\n```php\nuse Illuminate\\Contracts\\Database\\Eloquent\\CastsAttributes;\n\nclass JsonBase64Cast implements CastsAttributes {\n    public function get($model, string $key, $value, array $attributes): mixed {\n        return $value ? json_decode(base64_decode($value), true) : null;\n    }\n\n    public function set($model, string $key, $value, array $attributes): mixed {\n        return $value ? base64_encode(json_encode($value)) : null;\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1752, 'How do lockForUpdate() and sharedLock() prevent race conditions during checkout operations?', '### Pessimistic Locking\n\n```php\nDB::transaction(function () use ($productId, $qty) {\n    // Acquires exclusive row lock (FOR UPDATE):\n    $product = Product::where(\'id\', $productId)->lockForUpdate()->firstOrFail();\n    \n    if ($product->stock < $qty) throw new OutOfStockException();\n    \n    $product->decrement(\'stock\', $qty);\n    Order::create([\'product_id\' => $productId, \'quantity\' => $qty]);\n});\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1753, 'How do you define modern PHP 8 Attribute Accessors and Mutators in Laravel 9/10/11?', '### Modern Attribute Casts\n\n```php\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Casts\\Attribute;\n\nclass User extends Model {\n    protected function email(): Attribute {\n        return Attribute::make(\n            get: fn (string $val) => strtolower($val),\n            set: fn (string $val) => strtolower(trim($val)),\n        );\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1751, 'How do you handle automatic deadlock retry loops with DB::transaction() in Laravel?', '### Deadlock Retries in DB::transaction\n\n```php\n$result = DB::transaction(function () use ($fromId, $toId, $amount) {\n    Account::where(\'id\', $fromId)->decrement(\'balance\', $amount);\n    Account::where(\'id\', $toId)->increment(\'balance\', $amount);\n    return TransferLog::create(compact(\'fromId\', \'toId\', \'amount\'));\n}, 5); // 2nd parameter: max retry attempts on deadlock\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1750, 'What are the memory and performance differences between lazy(), lazyById(), and cursor() in Laravel?', '### lazy() vs cursor()\n\n- `cursor()`: Uses PDO unbuffered queries. Fetches 1 row at a time from MySQL with minimum RAM. Cannot execute other queries on same connection during iteration.\n- `lazy()` / `lazyById()`: Returns a `LazyCollection` fed by chunks behind the scenes. Safe for executing secondary queries during iteration.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1749, 'Why can chunk() skip records during batch updates and why must chunkById() be used instead?', '### chunk() vs chunkById()\n\n`chunk()` uses `LIMIT offset, limit`. If you update records in the loop (e.g. `is_processed = true`), the offset advances over rows that shifted, skipping half the dataset.\n\n`chunkById()` tracks `WHERE id > last_seen_id ORDER BY id ASC`, ensuring zero skipped rows even during massive updates.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1748, 'How does withExists() work in Laravel and what SQL subquery does it generate?', '### withExists()\n\nAdds a boolean attribute indicating whether related records exist without loading the relation records.\n\n```php\n$posts = Post::withExists(\'comments\')->get();\n\nif ($posts->first()->comments_exists) {\n    echo \'Post has comments!\';\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1747, 'How do you load relationship aggregates using withCount(), withSum(), withAvg(), withMin(), and withMax()?', '### Eager Loading Aggregates\n\n```php\n$books = Book::withCount(\'reviews\')\n             ->withAvg(\'reviews\', \'rating\')\n             ->withSum(\'orders\', \'quantity\')\n             ->withMax(\'orders\', \'price\')\n             ->get();\n\necho $books->first()->reviews_count;\necho $books->first()->reviews_avg_rating;\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1745, 'What checks does Model::shouldBeStrict() enable across Eloquent models in Laravel 9+?', '### Model::shouldBeStrict()\n\nEnables 3 critical safeguards:\n1. `preventLazyLoading()`: Catches N+1 queries.\n2. `preventSilentlyDiscardingAttributes()`: Throws exception when filling attributes not in `$fillable`.\n3. `preventAccessingMissingAttributes()`: Throws exception when accessing an attribute not selected in SQL query.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1746, 'How do has(), whereHas(), doesntHave(), and whereDoesntHave() work in Eloquent queries?', '### Relationship Existence Queries\n\n```php\n// Posts with at least 1 comment:\n$posts = Post::has(\'comments\')->get();\n\n// Posts with 3 or more comments:\n$posts = Post::has(\'comments\', \'>=\', 3)->get();\n\n// Posts with approved comments from specific authors:\n$posts = Post::whereHas(\'comments\', fn($q) => $q->where(\'approved\', true)->where(\'author_id\', 1))->get();\n\n// Users who have NEVER placed an order:\n$users = User::doesntHave(\'orders\')->get();\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1744, 'How do you configure Laravel to throw exceptions whenever lazy loading occurs during local testing?', '### Prevent Lazy Loading\n\n```php\n// In AppServiceProvider::boot():\npublic function boot(): void {\n    Model::preventLazyLoading(!app()->isProduction());\n    \n    Model::handleLazyLoadingViolationUsing(function ($model, $relation) {\n        logger()->warning(\"N+1 Lazy loading detected on [{$relation}] for model [\" . get_class($model) . \"]\");\n    });\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1743, 'What is the difference between load() and loadMissing() in Laravel?', '### load() vs loadMissing()\n\n- `load(\'comments\')`: Always executes a query to load comments, even if already loaded in memory.\n- `loadMissing(\'comments\')`: Checks if the relation is already loaded; only queries the database if comments are missing from memory.', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1740, 'How do you define dynamic latestOfMany() and oldestOfMany() one-to-one subquery relationships in Laravel?', '### Dynamic ofMany Relationships\n\n```php\nclass Customer extends Model {\n    // Latest order subquery join:\n    public function latestOrder(): HasOne {\n        return $this->hasOne(Order::class)->latestOfMany();\n    }\n\n    // Highest priced order subquery join:\n    public function largestOrder(): HasOne {\n        return $this->hasOne(Order::class)->ofMany(\'price\', \'max\');\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1741, 'What causes the N+1 query problem in Laravel, and how do you diagnose and eliminate it using with()?', '### Diagnosing and Eliminating N+1\n\nN+1 happens when loading relationships inside a loop, executing 1 query for parents + N queries for children.\n\n```php\n// Bad (101 queries):\n$books = Book::all();\nforeach ($books as $b) { echo $b->author->name; }\n\n// Good (2 queries):\n$books = Book::with(\'author\')->get();\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1742, 'How do you perform constrained eager loading with closures in Laravel?', '### Constrained Eager Loading\n\n```php\n$users = User::with([\n    \'posts\' => function ($query) {\n        $query->where(\'published\', true)\n              ->orderBy(\'published_at\', \'desc\')\n              ->limit(5);\n    },\n    \'posts.comments\' => function ($query) {\n        $query->where(\'is_approved\', true);\n    }\n])->get();\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1739, 'Why should you always call Relation::enforceMorphMap() in production, and how do you configure it?', '### Enforce Morph Map\n\nBy default, Laravel stores the full class namespace (e.g. `App\\Models\\Post`) in `commentable_type`. Renaming or moving the class breaks all database relationships.\n\n```php\n// In AppServiceProvider::boot():\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\Relation;\n\nRelation::enforceMorphMap([\n    \'post\' => \\App\\Models\\Post::class,\n    \'video\' => \\App\\Models\\Video::class,\n    \'product\' => \\App\\Models\\Product::class,\n]);\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1738, 'How do you implement a universal Tagging system using morphToMany and morphedByMany?', '### Polymorphic Many-to-Many\n\n```php\nclass Tag extends Model {\n    public function posts(): MorphedByMany {\n        return $this->morphedByMany(Post::class, \'taggable\');\n    }\n\n    public function videos(): MorphedByMany {\n        return $this->morphedByMany(Video::class, \'taggable\');\n    }\n}\n\nclass Post extends Model {\n    public function tags(): MorphToMany {\n        return $this->morphToMany(Tag::class, \'taggable\');\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1736, 'How do you implement a Polymorphic One-to-One relationship for User and Post avatars/images?', '### Polymorphic One-to-One\n\n```php\nclass Image extends Model {\n    public function imageable(): MorphTo {\n        return $this->morphTo();\n    }\n}\n\nclass User extends Model {\n    public function avatar(): MorphOne {\n        return $this->morphOne(Image::class, \'imageable\');\n    }\n}\n\nclass Post extends Model {\n    public function featuredImage(): MorphOne {\n        return $this->morphOne(Image::class, \'imageable\');\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1737, 'How do you build a unified Commenting system across Posts, Videos, and Products using morphMany?', '### Polymorphic One-to-Many\n\n```php\nclass Comment extends Model {\n    public function commentable(): MorphTo {\n        return $this->morphTo();\n    }\n}\n\nclass Video extends Model {\n    public function comments(): MorphMany {\n        return $this->morphMany(Comment::class, \'commentable\');\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1735, 'How do you configure a hasManyThrough relationship to fetch all deployments for an environment through projects?', '### Has Many Through\n\n```php\nclass Environment extends Model {\n    public function deployments(): HasManyThrough {\n        return $this->hasManyThrough(\n            Deployment::class, // Final model\n            Project::class,    // Intermediate model\n            \'environment_id\',  // FK on Project table\n            \'project_id\',      // FK on Deployment table\n            \'id\',              // Local key on Environment table\n            \'id\'               // Local key on Project table\n        );\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1734, 'How does hasOneThrough work in Laravel and what is a practical real-world use case?', '### Has One Through\n\nhasOneThrough accesses a distant single model through an intermediate model.\n\n```\n[Supplier] ---> [Account] ---> [AccountHistory]\n```\n\n```php\nclass Supplier extends Model {\n    public function accountHistory(): HasOneThrough {\n        return $this->hasOneThrough(\n            AccountHistory::class, // Final model\n            Account::class,        // Intermediate model\n            \'supplier_id\',         // Foreign key on Account table\n            \'account_id\',          // Foreign key on AccountHistory table\n            \'id\',                  // Local key on Supplier table\n            \'id\'                   // Local key on Account table\n        );\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1733, 'How do you configure a Many-to-Many relationship with extra pivot columns, timestamps, and custom pivot model?', '### Many to Many with Custom Pivot Model\n\n```php\nclass User extends Model {\n    public function roles(): BelongsToMany {\n        return $this->belongsToMany(Role::class, \'role_user\', \'user_id\', \'role_id\')\n                    ->withPivot(\'assigned_by\', \'expires_at\')\n                    ->withTimestamps()\n                    ->using(RoleUserPivot::class)\n                    ->as(\'membership\');\n    }\n}\n\n// Custom Pivot Model:\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\Pivot;\n\nclass RoleUserPivot extends Pivot {\n    protected $casts = [\n        \'expires_at\' => \'datetime\',\n    ];\n\n    public function isExpired(): bool {\n        return $this->expires_at->isPast();\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1731, 'How do you define and configure a One-to-One relationship in Laravel with custom foreign and local keys?', '### One to One Relationship\n\n```php\nclass User extends Model {\n    public function phone(): HasOne {\n        // hasOne(RelatedModel, foreign_key, local_key)\n        return $this->hasOne(Phone::class, \'user_id\', \'id\');\n    }\n}\n\nclass Phone extends Model {\n    public function user(): BelongsTo {\n        // belongsTo(RelatedModel, foreign_key, owner_key)\n        return $this->belongsTo(User::class, \'user_id\', \'id\');\n    }\n}\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1732, 'How do you define a One-to-Many relationship and insert related models using save() vs create()?', '### One to Many Creation\n\n```php\n$user = User::find(1);\n\n// 1. Using create() with mass assignment attributes\n$post = $user->posts()->create([\n    \'title\' => \'Understanding Eloquent\',\n    \'content\' => \'Deep dive into relations...\'\n]);\n\n// 2. Using save() with an existing model instance\n$comment = new Comment([\'body\' => \'Great explanation!\']);\n$post->comments()->save($comment);\n\n// 3. createMany() for batch insertions\n$user->posts()->createMany([\n    [\'title\' => \'Post 1\', \'content\' => \'Content 1\'],\n    [\'title\' => \'Post 2\', \'content\' => \'Content 2\'],\n]);\n```', 'Laravel Database & Eloquent Mastery'),
(1917, 'What is the Empty Base Optimization (EBO) and how does [[no_unique_address]] in C++20 replace it?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nEBO allows 0-byte base classes to consume 0 bytes in derived classes. [[no_unique_address]] allows empty member variables to take 0 bytes without inheritance.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1918, 'How does std::shared_mutex enable reader-writer locking in C++17?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nAllows multiple concurrent shared readers (shared_lock) while guaranteeing exclusive access for writers (unique_lock).\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1919, 'What is the Strict Aliasing Rule in C++ and how does std::bit_cast in C++20 safely reinterpret binary types?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nCompiler assumes pointers of different types never point to the same memory. std::bit_cast copies bits safely without undefined behavior.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1920, 'What are Structured Bindings in C++17 and how do you customize tuple_size and get for custom structs?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nauto [a, b] = my_pair; unpacks tuples, arrays, and structs. Custom types implement std::tuple_size and get<I>() to support structured binding.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1921, 'How does type erasure work in std::function vs custom lightweight type-erased callable wrappers?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nstd::function uses virtual calls and potential heap allocations; custom type erasers use function pointer vtables for zero-overhead dispatch.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1922, 'What is the CRTP (Curiously Recurring Template Pattern) and how does it achieve static polymorphism in C++?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nClass Derived : public Base<Derived> resolves polymorphic method calls at compile time without vtable pointer overhead.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1923, 'How does std::format in C++20 replace printf and std::stringstream with type-safe high-speed formatting?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nProvides Python-like formatting syntax ({:02x}), compile-time format string validation, and superior performance compared to iostreams.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1924, 'How does std::expected in C++23 provide monadic error handling without exceptions?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nHolds either an expected value or an unexpected error (std::unexpected), supporting and_then(), transform(), and or_else() pipelines.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1925, 'What is SIMD vectorization and how do compiler intrinsics and std::experimental::simd accelerate computational throughput in C++?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nProcesses multiple data elements in a single CPU instruction (AVX2, AVX-512), dramatically improving numeric algorithm throughput.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1926, 'What are the differences between constexpr, consteval, and constinit in C++20?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nconstexpr evaluates at compile or runtime; consteval forces compile-time execution (immediate functions); constinit guarantees compile-time variable initialization without dynamic initialization order fiasco.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1927, 'How does std::jthread in C++20 improve thread lifecycle management over std::thread?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nstd::jthread automatically joins upon destruction and supports cooperative cancellation tokens via std::stop_token.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1928, 'Explain C++ Memory Orders (memory_order_relaxed, memory_order_acquire, memory_order_release, memory_order_seq_cst) in std::atomic.', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nControls instruction reordering across threads. Relaxed guarantees atomicity only; Acquire-Release synchronizes read/write boundaries; Seq_Cst enforces global sequential consistency.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1929, 'What is False Sharing in multithreaded C++ programs and how does alignas(hardware_destructive_interference_size) prevent it?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nOccurs when independent variables reside on the same CPU cache line (64 bytes), causing cache invalidation ping-pong. alignas pads variables to separate cache lines.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1930, 'How does std::string_view provide zero-copy string slicing and what are the lifetime dangling pitfalls?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nStores pointer and length without allocating heap memory; dangling occurs if the underlying owner string is destroyed before the view.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1931, 'What is std::span in C++20 and how does it replace raw buffer pointer-length function arguments?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nA non-owning view over contiguous sequences (arrays, vectors) providing bounds safety and uniform APIs.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1932, 'How does std::variant implement type-safe discriminated unions and how do you use std::visit with the overloaded pattern?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nstd::variant holds one of several types safely without heap allocations. std::visit dispatches pattern matching using custom lambda overloads.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1933, 'How does std::optional avoid sentinel values and represent nullable data cleanly in modern C++?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nHolds a value or std::nullopt without dynamic allocation, with methods like value_or(), and monadic operations (and_then, transform in C++23).\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1934, 'What is Copy Elision, Return Value Optimization (RVO), and Named RVO (NRVO) in C++17?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nConstructs the returned object directly in the caller\'s storage; C++17 makes prvalue copy elision mandatory by language standard.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1935, 'What are Fold Expressions in C++17 variadic templates?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nAllows unpacking variadic template parameter packs over binary operators like (... + args) or (... && predicates) with concise syntax.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1936, 'How does std::is_invocable and std::invoke work across member function pointers, free functions, and lambdas?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nstd::invoke provides a unified calling mechanism regardless of whether the callable is a lambda, functor, free function, or member pointer.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1937, 'What is the Empty Base Optimization (EBO) and how does [[no_unique_address]] in C++20 replace it?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nEBO allows 0-byte base classes to consume 0 bytes in derived classes. [[no_unique_address]] allows empty member variables to take 0 bytes without inheritance.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1938, 'How does std::shared_mutex enable reader-writer locking in C++17?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nAllows multiple concurrent shared readers (shared_lock) while guaranteeing exclusive access for writers (unique_lock).\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1939, 'What is the Strict Aliasing Rule in C++ and how does std::bit_cast in C++20 safely reinterpret binary types?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nCompiler assumes pointers of different types never point to the same memory. std::bit_cast copies bits safely without undefined behavior.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1940, 'What are Structured Bindings in C++17 and how do you customize tuple_size and get for custom structs?', '### Advanced Modern C++ Engineering\n\nauto [a, b] = my_pair; unpacks tuples, arrays, and structs. Custom types implement std::tuple_size and get<I>() to support structured binding.\n\n#### Core Principle:\nLeverage zero-cost abstractions, RAII resource management, and compile-time type safety.', 'Advanced Modern C++'),
(1941, 'How does the Cost-Based Optimizer (CBO) estimate query execution plans using database statistics and histograms?', '### Cost-Based Optimizer (CBO) & Histograms\n\nThe Cost-Based Optimizer analyzes table statistics (cardinality, row counts, page counts, and data distribution **histograms**) to evaluate multiple potential execution trees and choose the plan with the lowest estimated I/O and CPU cost.\n\n- **Equi-Width Histograms**: Divides range of values into equal intervals.\n- **Equi-Depth (Height-Balanced) Histograms**: Divides data such that each bucket holds approximately the same number of rows, accurately estimating skewed data distributions (e.g., 90% of rows have `status = \'completed\'`).', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1942, 'What are the internal differences and performance trade-offs between Nested Loop Join, Hash Join, and Merge Sort Join?', '### Join Algorithms Internals\n\n```\n+------------------+------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| Join Algorithm   | Best Scenario                | Complexity                        |\n+------------------+------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| Nested Loop Join | Small outer table + Index on | O(N * log M)                      |\n|                  | inner table                  |                                   |\n| Hash Join        | Large unsorted tables with   | O(N + M) (Builds hash table in    |\n|                  | equi-joins                   | memory for smaller relation)      |\n| Merge Sort Join  | Large pre-sorted datasets    | O(N + M) (or O(N log N + M log M) |\n|                  | on join keys                 | if sorting is required)           |\n+------------------+------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n```', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1943, 'How does Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) and the Redo/Undo log architecture guarantee ACID durability and crash recovery?', '### Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) & Crash Recovery\n\nRelational engines never write modified data pages directly to table disk files on every transaction (which causes random I/O bottlenecks). Instead:\n\n1. **Redo Log (WAL)**: Records sequential physical/logical changes before committing. In crash scenarios, the engine replays the Redo log (**Roll-Forward**) to recover committed pages.\n2. **Undo Log**: Stores previous versions of modified records to support rollback on abort (**Roll-Back**) and power MVCC consistent non-blocking reads.\n3. **Checkpointing**: Periodically flushes dirty in-memory buffer pool pages to disk, bounding the recovery replay time.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1944, 'How do LATERAL derived tables (Cross Apply / Lateral Joins) work in Advanced SQL and what problems do they solve?', '### LATERAL Joins (Cross Apply)\n\nA standard subquery in `FROM` cannot reference columns from preceding tables in the same `FROM` clause. A **`LATERAL` join** acts as a `for-each` loop in SQL, evaluating the subquery for each row of the outer table.\n\n```sql\n-- Fetch top 3 latest orders for EVERY customer in a single high-speed query:\nSELECT c.name, o.id AS order_id, o.amount, o.created_at\nFROM customers c\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    SELECT id, amount, created_at\n    FROM orders\n    WHERE customer_id = c.id\n    ORDER BY created_at DESC\n    LIMIT 3\n) o;\n```', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1945, 'How do you solve classic Gap and Island problems in SQL using window functions?', '### The Gap and Island Problem in SQL\n\n- **Island**: Contiguous sequences of data (e.g. consecutive active days, continuous room bookings).\n- **Gap**: Missing periods or intervals between sequences.\n\n#### Solving Islands using `ROW_NUMBER()` Difference:\n```sql\nWITH Numbered AS (\n    SELECT \n        user_id,\n        login_date,\n        login_date - INTERVAL ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date) DAY AS grp\n    FROM user_logins\n)\nSELECT \n    user_id,\n    MIN(login_date) AS streak_start,\n    MAX(login_date) AS streak_end,\n    COUNT(*) AS consecutive_days\nFROM Numbered\nGROUP BY user_id, grp;\n```', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1946, 'What are B+Tree page splits, fragmentation, and how does the Fill Factor setting mitigate write amplification?', '### B+Tree Page Splits & Fill Factor\n\nWhen inserting into a full B+Tree leaf page (typically 16KB in MySQL InnoDB or 8KB in Postgres), the database must allocate a new page and move roughly 50% of the rows to the new page. This causes **Page Splits**, random I/O, and physical index fragmentation.\n\n- **Fill Factor**: Sets the percentage of space on each page left empty during index creation (e.g., Fill Factor = 80%). This reserve space accommodates future updates/inserts without triggering immediate page splits.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1947, 'How do Next-Key Locks and Gap Locks in InnoDB prevent Phantom Reads under the Repeatable Read isolation level?', '### Next-Key Locks & Phantom Read Prevention\n\nUnder ANSI SQL, `REPEATABLE READ` can suffer from **Phantom Reads** (a concurrent transaction inserts a new row that matches a range query filter).\n\n```\nIndex values: [ 10 ] -------- Gap 1 -------- [ 20 ] -------- Gap 2 -------- [ 30 ]\n```\n\n- **Record Lock**: Locks the index record itself (e.g., locks 20).\n- **Gap Lock**: Locks the open gap between index records (e.g., locks range `(10, 20)`).\n- **Next-Key Lock**: Combines a Gap Lock with a Record Lock on the trailing record (`(10, 20]`). Any attempt by another transaction to insert a row with key 15 is blocked until the transaction commits.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1948, 'What is the difference between Inverted Indexes (GIN/GiST) and standard B+Tree indexes for semi-structured and full-text data?', '### Inverted Indexes (GIN / GiST) vs B+Tree\n\n- **B+Tree**: Maps a single scalar value to a row locator. Excellent for range queries (`>`, `<`) and exact equality (`=`).\n- **GIN (Generalized Inverted Index)**: Maps individual internal elements (tokens, array items, JSON keys) to multiple row IDs. Crucial for JSONB containment (`@>`), array search (`&&`), and Full-Text search.\n- **GiST (Generalized Search Tree)**: Lossy balanced tree structure essential for spatial multidimensional data (bounding boxes, GIS geometry).', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1949, 'How does Consistent Hashing work in distributed SQL databases for horizontal data sharding?', '### Consistent Hashing in Distributed SQL\n\nTraditional modulo hashing (`hash(key) % N`) forces rehashing almost all keys whenever nodes are added or removed. **Consistent Hashing** maps both data keys and database nodes to a circular hash ring (e.g. 0 to $2^{32}-1$).\n\n```\n               Node A (Tokens: 0 - 1000)\n              /                         \\\n   Node C (2001 - 3000)               Node B (1001 - 2000)\n```\n\nWhen a new node joins the cluster, it only takes ownership of a fraction of keys from adjacent nodes, minimizing data migration traffic.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1950, 'How do you perform Online Schema Changes (Zero-Downtime Migrations) on massive tables with millions of rows?', '### Zero-Downtime Online Schema Changes\n\nExecuting `ALTER TABLE large_table ADD COLUMN ...` can lock tables and stall traffic.\n\n#### Trigger-Based Ghost Pattern (e.g., `gh-ost`, `pt-online-schema-change`):\n1. Create a shadow table `_large_table_new` with the desired new schema.\n2. Backfill historical records from old to shadow table in micro-batches.\n3. Stream incremental live DML changes via binary log (`gh-ost`) or triggers (`pt-osc`).\n4. Execute an atomic table rename: `RENAME TABLE large_table TO _old, _large_table_new TO large_table;` with zero downtime.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1951, 'What are the differences between RANGE BETWEEN and ROWS BETWEEN in window frame specifications?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nROWS counts physical rows; RANGE calculates logical value offsets based on the ORDER BY value.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1952, 'How does the Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) visibility map and VACUUM operate in PostgreSQL?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nMaintains dead tuples from updates/deletes; VACUUM reclaims storage and updates the visibility map to enable Index-Only Scans.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1953, 'What is an Index Skip Scan and how does the optimizer execute queries without leading index columns?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nSkips through distinct values of the low-cardinality leading column to utilize the secondary indexed column efficiently.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1954, 'What is the difference between Optimistic Concurrency Control and Two-Phase Locking (2PL)?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nOCC validates on commit without locking; 2PL acquires growing locks in phase 1 and releases shrinking locks in phase 2.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1955, 'How does Bitmap Index Scan combine multiple secondary indexes in an execution plan?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nConverts matching rows from multiple indexes into in-memory bitmaps and performs bitwise AND/OR operations before reading data pages.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1956, 'How do Partial (Filtered) Indexes minimize storage and increase cache hit ratios?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nIndexes only rows satisfying a predicate (e.g. WHERE status = \'unprocessed\'), saving RAM and write overhead.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1957, 'How does Two-Phase Commit (2PC) maintain atomic transactions across distributed database shards?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nCoordinator sends Prepare phase to all participants; if all vote yes, sends Commit phase; otherwise sends Abort.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `question`, `answer`, `category`) VALUES
(1958, 'What are the mechanics of Lock Escalation and how to prevent table-level locking in high-throughput workloads?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nDatabase converts thousands of fine-grained row locks into a single table lock when memory thresholds are exceeded.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1959, 'How do Common Table Expressions (CTEs) behave when declared MATERIALIZED vs NOT MATERIALIZED?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nMATERIALIZED evaluates the CTE once as an internal temporary table; NOT MATERIALIZED inlines the CTE into the main query plan.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1960, 'What are Adaptive Hash Indexes in MySQL InnoDB and how do they speed up B+Tree lookups?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nInnoDB automatically builds in-memory hash indexes on frequently accessed B+Tree leaf pages for O(1) buffer lookups.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1961, 'How do You pivot dynamic rows to columns using conditional aggregation in SQL?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nUse SELECT category, SUM(CASE WHEN month = 1 THEN sales ELSE 0 END) AS jan_sales GROUP BY category.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1962, 'How does Partition Pruning eliminate disk scans in partitioned database tables?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nQuery optimizer inspects WHERE clauses and skips scanning partition files that cannot contain matching data.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1963, 'What is the difference between Synchronous, Semi-Synchronous, and Asynchronous Database Replication?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nAsync returns immediately after primary commit; Semi-sync waits for at least 1 replica ack; Sync waits for all replicas.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1964, 'How do Covering Indexes achieve Index-Only Scans with zero heap/table access?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nAll columns in SELECT, JOIN, and WHERE exist within the index leaf nodes, answering queries directly from index buffers.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1965, 'What causes Deadlocks in distributed SQL environments and how does a Wait-For Graph resolve them?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nTransactions form circular lock dependencies; the database detects cycles in the Wait-For Graph and terminates the lowest-cost transaction.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
(1966, 'What are the differences between RANGE BETWEEN and ROWS BETWEEN in window frame specifications?', '### Advanced SQL & Database Internals\n\nROWS counts physical rows; RANGE calculates logical value offsets based on the ORDER BY value.\n\n#### Engineering Guideline:\nAnalyze query execution plans with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and design indexes matching the underlying storage engine.', 'Advanced SQL & Database Internals'),
