(2046, 'What are React Server Components (RSC) in Next.js App Router, and how do they differ fundamentally from Client Components?', '### React Server Components (RSC) vs Client Components\n\n```\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Feature               | Server Component (Default)  | Client Component (\'use client\')|\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Execution Location    | Server ONLY (Build/Request) | Server (Prerender) + Client |\n| Bundle Size Impact    | 0 KB JavaScript sent to browser | Increases Client JS bundle  |\n| Access Backend/DB     | Direct (Prisma, SQL, FS, API)| Needs API / Server Action   |\n| React Hooks & State   | Cannot use useState/useEffect | Can use all React Hooks     |\n| Browser APIs          | Cannot access window/localStorage | Full access in useEffect    |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\n- **RSC**: Rendered to a special JSON format on the server and streamed to the browser without increasing client JavaScript bundles.\n- **Client Components**: Defined with `\'use client\'` at top of file, allowing interactivity and event listeners (`onClick`).', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2047, 'How do the 4 distinct caching layers in Next.js App Router work and how do you configure each?', '### The 4 Caching Mechanisms in Next.js\n\n```\n+----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Cache Layer          | Where it Lives              | What it Caches              | How to Invalidate / Purge   |\n+----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Request Memoization  | Server Memory (Per-request) | Duplicate fetch() GET calls | Automatic per render cycle  |\n| Data Cache           | Server Persistent Storage   | fetch() HTTP responses      | revalidateTag / Path        |\n| Full Route Cache     | Server Persistent Storage   | Rendered HTML & RSC Payload | Build time / Revalidation   |\n| Router Cache         | Client Browser Memory       | RSC payloads of visited pages| router.refresh() / Time     |\n+----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\n#### Fetch Caching Options:\n```ts\n// 1. Force Cache (Default SSG):\nfetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\', { cache: \'force-cache\' });\n\n// 2. Dynamic (No Cache / SSR):\nfetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\', { cache: \'no-store\' });\n\n// 3. Time-based Revalidation (ISR):\nfetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\', { next: { revalidate: 60 } }); // 60 seconds\n\n// 4. Tag-based Revalidation:\nfetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\', { next: { tags: [\'products\'] } });\n```', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2048, 'How do Server Actions work in Next.js 14/15, and how do you handle form submission, validation, and optimistic updates?', '### Server Actions & Optimistic UI\n\nServer Actions are asynchronous functions executed on the server, invoked from forms or event handlers without writing API endpoints.\n\n```tsx\n// app/actions/create-post.ts\n\'use server\';\n\nimport { revalidateTag } from \'next/cache\';\nimport { z } from \'zod\';\nimport { db } from \'@/lib/db\';\n\nconst PostSchema = z.object({\n    title: z.string().min(3),\n    content: z.string().min(10),\n});\n\nexport async function createPost(prevState: any, formData: FormData) {\n    const parsed = PostSchema.safeParse({\n        title: formData.get(\'title\'),\n        content: formData.get(\'content\'),\n    });\n\n    if (!parsed.success) {\n        return { errors: parsed.error.flatten().fieldErrors };\n    }\n\n    await db.post.create({ data: parsed.data });\n    revalidateTag(\'posts\');\n    return { success: true };\n}\n```', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2049, 'What are Parallel Routes (@slot) and Intercepting Routes ((.)route) in Next.js, and how do you build a modal gallery?', '### Parallel & Intercepting Routes\n\n- **Parallel Routes (`@slot`)**: Render multiple pages simultaneously in the same layout (e.g. `@analytics`, `@feed`).\n- **Intercepting Routes (`(.)photo/[id]`)**: Intercepts a route to display an in-context modal while preserving the shareable URL when refreshed.\n\n```\napp/\n  layout.tsx\n  page.tsx\n  @modal/                     <-- Parallel slot\n    (.)photos/[id]/           <-- Intercepting route (modal view)\n      page.tsx\n  photos/[id]/                <-- Full standalone page on hard refresh\n    page.tsx\n```', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2050, 'How does Streaming with Suspense and `loading.tsx` achieve Progressive Hydration in the App Router?', '### Streaming with Suspense\n\nNext.js streams server-rendered HTML chunks as they become available, improving Time to First Byte (TTFB) and First Contentful Paint (FCP).\n\n```tsx\nimport { Suspense } from \'react\';\nimport { ProductDetails, ProductReviews, ReviewsSkeleton } from \'@/components\';\n\nexport default async function ProductPage({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {\n    return (\n        <main>\n            {/* Instant Render */}\n            <ProductDetails id={params.id} />\n            \n            {/* Slow reviews component streams in when ready */}\n            <Suspense fallback={<ReviewsSkeleton />}>\n                <ProductReviews productId={params.id} />\n            </Suspense>\n        </main>\n    );\n}\n```', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2051, 'What is Partial Prerendering (PPR) in Next.js 14/15 and how does it combine Static Shell with Dynamic Holes?', '### Partial Prerendering (PPR)\n\nPPR enables rendering a static shell at build time while keeping dynamic components streamed on request within the same page—without requiring client-side `fetch()`.\n\n```\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Static Shell (Prerendered at build time - Served from Edge) |\n|  [Navbar]  [Header Banner]  [Sidebar Links]                 |\n|                                                             |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  | Dynamic Streamed Hole (Suspense Fallback -> User Cart)|  |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------+  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n```tsx\nexport const experimental_ppr = true;\n```', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2052, 'How do Route Handlers (`app/api/route.ts`) work in the App Router, and how do you stream SSE or AI responses?', '### Route Handlers & AI Streaming\n\nRoute Handlers use standard Web `Request` and `Response` objects.\n\n```ts\n// app/api/chat/route.ts\nimport { OpenAIStream, StreamingTextResponse } from \'ai\';\n\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n    const { messages } = await req.json();\n    const response = await openai.chat.completions.create({\n        model: \'gpt-4o\',\n        stream: true,\n        messages,\n    });\n    \n    const stream = OpenAIStream(response);\n    return new StreamingTextResponse(stream);\n}\n```', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2053, 'How does `generateStaticParams()` replace `getStaticPaths()` in Next.js App Router for Static Site Generation (SSG)?', '### `generateStaticParams()`\n\n`generateStaticParams()` generates static route segments at build time for dynamic routes (`[slug]`).\n\n```tsx\n// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx\nexport async function generateStaticParams() {\n    const posts = await db.post.findMany({ select: { slug: true } });\n    \n    return posts.map((post) => ({\n        slug: post.slug,\n    }));\n}\n\nexport default async function BlogPostPage({ params }: { params: { slug: string } }) {\n    const post = await getPostBySlug(params.slug);\n    return <article><h1>{post.title}</h1></article>;\n}\n```', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2054, 'How does Middleware (`middleware.ts`) operate on the Edge runtime and how do you implement authentication redirects?', '### Next.js Edge Middleware\n\nMiddleware runs before cached content and route handlers, allowing header modifications, geolocation routing, and auth checks.\n\n```ts\n// middleware.ts\nimport { NextResponse } from \'next/server\';\nimport type { NextRequest } from \'next/server\';\n\nexport function middleware(request: NextRequest) {\n    const token = request.cookies.get(\'auth_token\')?.value;\n\n    if (!token && request.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith(\'/dashboard\')) {\n        const loginUrl = new URL(\'/login\', request.url);\n        loginUrl.searchParams.set(\'from\', request.nextUrl.pathname);\n        return NextResponse.redirect(loginUrl);\n    }\n\n    return NextResponse.next();\n}\n\nexport const config = {\n    matcher: [\'/dashboard/:path*\', \'/settings/:path*\'],\n};\n```', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `question`, `answer`, `category`) VALUES
(2055, 'How does Next.js optimize Core Web Vitals using `<Image />`, `next/font`, and the Metadata API?', '### Next.js Performance Optimizations\n\n1. **`next/image`**: Automatic WebP/AVIF conversion, responsive `srcset`, lazy loading, and prevention of Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS).\n2. **`next/font`**: Zero layout shift by inlining font CSS and self-hosting Google fonts automatically.\n3. **Metadata API**: Generates static and dynamic Open Graph, Twitter Cards, and canonical tags for SEO.\n\n```tsx\nexport async function generateMetadata({ params }): Promise<Metadata> {\n    const product = await getProduct(params.id);\n    return {\n        title: product.name,\n        description: product.summary,\n        openGraph: {\n            images: [product.imageUrl],\n        },\n    };\n}\n```', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2056, 'What is the difference between layouts.tsx and templates.tsx in Next.js App Router?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nLayouts preserve state and do not re-render on navigation between sibling routes; Templates create a new instance and reset state/effects on every navigation.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2057, 'How does Route Group syntax (group) organize folders without affecting the URL structure?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nWrapping folder names in parentheses e.g. (marketing) or (dashboard) groups routes logically without adding /marketing to the URL path.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2058, 'How do you prevent sensitive backend code leaks using the server-only package in Next.js?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nImporting \'server-only\' in a utility or database file throws a build-time compile error if accidentally imported into a Client Component.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2059, 'How do revalidatePath() and revalidateTag() purge Next.js Data Cache on-demand?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nrevalidateTag(\'products\') invalidates all cached fetch requests tagged with \'products\'; revalidatePath(\'/blog\') revalidates specific route path caches.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2060, 'What are the differences between dynamicParams true and false in dynamic App Router segments?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nWhen dynamicParams is false, visiting a dynamic route not generated by generateStaticParams returns a 404 instead of rendering on-demand.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2061, 'How does the useOptimistic hook provide instant UI feedback during Server Action execution?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nDisplays optimistic state updates immediately while awaiting server response; automatically rolls back if the server action throws an error.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2062, 'How do useFormStatus and useActionState (useFormState) manage form pending states in Next.js?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nuseFormStatus exposes pending boolean to submit buttons; useActionState manages action return state and error messages.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2063, 'How to configure custom HTTP headers, CSP, and CORS in Next.js next.config.js?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nDefine async headers() returning source pattern and key-value arrays for Content-Security-Policy and CORS headers.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2064, 'What is the difference between unstable_cache and standard fetch caching in Next.js?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nunstable_cache wraps arbitrary async database or ORM functions (e.g. Prisma queries) with Next.js Data Cache and tag revalidation.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2065, 'How do you implement internationalization (i18n) routing with App Router middleware?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nUse middleware to detect user Accept-Language headers and rewrite paths to localized route groups (e.g. /[lang]/about).\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2066, 'How does standalone Docker deployment (output: \'standalone\') optimize Next.js production builds?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nTraces node_modules and bundles only the necessary files into .next/standalone, reducing Docker image size by over 80%.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2067, 'What are the trade-offs between Edge Runtime and Node.js Runtime in Route Handlers?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nEdge runtime provides global low latency and instant cold starts with limited Node APIs; Node.js runtime provides full Node.js API support.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2068, 'How do catch-all ([...slug]) and optional catch-all ([[...slug]]) dynamic routes differ?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nCatch-all requires at least 1 segment (/docs/a); Optional catch-all also matches the root route (/docs) without parameters.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2069, 'How to handle global errors using global-error.tsx and not-found.tsx in Next.js?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nglobal-error.tsx catches errors in root layout; not-found.tsx renders custom 404 UI when notFound() is triggered.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2070, 'How does Turbopack accelerate local development and hot module reloading in Next.js?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nRust-based incremental bundler that caches function-level compilation results for sub-50ms HMR updates.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
(2071, 'What is the difference between layouts.tsx and templates.tsx in Next.js App Router?', '### Next.js App Router Architecture\n\nLayouts preserve state and do not re-render on navigation between sibling routes; Templates create a new instance and reset state/effects on every navigation.\n\n#### Best Practice:\nAdopt a Server-First mental model: keep components as Server Components by default, and push `\'use client\'` down to the interactive leaf nodes of your component tree.', 'Next.js App Router & Full-Stack Architecture'),
