INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `question`, `answer`, `category`) VALUES
(2151, 'What are the fundamental differences between BSON and JSON in MongoDB, and what is the 16MB document size limit?', '### BSON vs JSON & Document Limits\n\n```\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Feature               | JSON                        | BSON (Binary JSON)          |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Format                | Text (Human-readable)       | Binary representation       |\n| Data Types            | String, Number, Bool, Array | Date, Int32, Int64, Decimal128, |\n|                       | Object, Null                | Binary, ObjectId, Timestamp |\n| Traversal Speed       | Slow (Must parse entire str)| Fast (Prefix byte length headers)|\n| Storage Efficiency    | Verbose text                | Compact binary encoding     |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\n- **16MB Limit**: A single BSON document cannot exceed 16 Megabytes to prevent excessive RAM/network consumption. For larger binary data (videos, large PDFs), use **GridFS** which chunks files into 255KB chunks across `fs.files` and `fs.chunks` collections.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2152, 'How is a MongoDB ObjectId constructed and how can you extract its creation timestamp without querying extra fields?', '### MongoDB ObjectId Structure\n\nAn ObjectId is a **12-byte (24 hex characters)** unique identifier constructed as follows:\n\n```\n+------------------+------------------+------------------+\n| 4 Bytes          | 5 Bytes          | 3 Bytes          |\n| Unix Timestamp   | Random Process ID| Incrementing     |\n| (Seconds)        | & Machine Hash   | Counter          |\n+------------------+------------------+------------------+\n```\n\n#### Extracting Creation Timestamp in JavaScript:\n```js\nconst id = new ObjectId(\"64f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1\");\nconsole.log(id.getTimestamp()); // Output: 2026-08-19T14:32:00.000Z\n```', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2153, 'How do the array update operators `$push`, `$addToSet`, `$pull`, and positional operators `$[]` and `$[identifier]` work?', '### Advanced Array Update Operators\n\n```js\n// 1. $addToSet: Adds element ONLY if it does not already exist (sets uniqueness)\ndb.users.updateOne({ _id: userId }, { $addToSet: { tags: \"mongodb\" } });\n\n// 2. $push with $each, $slice, and $sort: Maintain top 5 highest scores\ndb.players.updateOne({ _id: id }, {\n    $push: {\n        scores: {\n            $each: [95, 88],\n            $sort: -1,\n            $slice: 5\n        }\n    }\n});\n\n// 3. Array Filters $[identifier]: Update specific elements matching conditions in nested array\ndb.orders.updateOne(\n    { _id: orderId },\n    { $set: { \"items.$[elem].discount\": 0.15 } },\n    { arrayFilters: [{ \"elem.price\": { $gt: 100 } }] }\n);\n```', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2154, 'What are the key differences between Embedding (Denormalization) and Referencing (Normalization) in MongoDB schema design?', '### Embedding vs Referencing Schema Rules\n\n```\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Pattern               | Embedding (Denormalization) | Referencing (Normalization) |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Performance           | Single read/write operation | Requires $lookup / joins    |\n| Atomicity             | Atomic document updates     | Multi-document transactions |\n| Growth Risk           | 16MB document limit overflow| Unbounded array growth safe |\n| Relationship Fit      | 1:1, 1:Few (e.g. 5-10 items)| 1:Many, 1:Squillions, N:M   |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Embed when**: Data is viewed together, updated together, and bounded in size (e.g. User addresses, Order line items).\n- **Reference when**: Data is accessed independently or frequently unbounded (e.g. User followers, Log events).', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2155, 'How does the Aggregation Framework execute multi-stage pipelines and how does `$lookup` perform joins in MongoDB?', '### MongoDB Aggregation Pipeline\n\nData passes through sequential transformation stages where each stage transforms documents as stream pipelines.\n\n```js\ndb.orders.aggregate([\n    // Stage 1: Filter\n    { $match: { status: \"completed\", createdAt: { $gte: new Date(\"2026-01-01\") } } },\n    \n    // Stage 2: Left Outer Join with Customers\n    { $lookup: {\n        from: \"customers\",\n        localField: \"customerId\",\n        foreignField: \"_id\",\n        as: \"customer\"\n    } },\n    { $unwind: \"$customer\" },\n    \n    // Stage 3: Group by Customer & Calculate Total Spend\n    { $group: {\n        _id: \"$customer.email\",\n        totalSpent: { $sum: \"$totalAmount\" },\n        orderCount: { $sum: 1 }\n    } },\n    \n    // Stage 4: Sort\n    { $sort: { totalSpent: -1 } }\n]);\n```', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2156, 'What is the ESR (Equality, Sort, Range) Rule and how does it determine the optimal order of fields in a Compound Index?', '### The ESR Indexing Rule\n\nWhen designing a compound index for queries with equality filters, sorting, and range conditions, always place fields in the index in this exact order:\n\n```\n[1. Equality Fields]  --->  [2. Sort Fields]  --->  [3. Range Fields]\n```\n\n#### Example Query:\n```js\ndb.orders.find({ status: \"active\", total: { $gte: 50, $lte: 200 } }).sort({ createdAt: -1 });\n```\n\n#### Optimal Compound Index:\n```js\n// E: status (equality) | S: createdAt (sort) | R: total (range)\ndb.orders.createIndex({ status: 1, createdAt: -1, total: 1 });\n```\n*Adhering to ESR avoids in-memory sorting (`Using filesort` / `SORT_KEY_GENERATOR`) and enables high-speed index bounds scans.*', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2157, 'How do you interpret MongoDB `explain(\"executionStats\")` output and diagnose COLLSCAN vs IXSCAN?', '### `explain(\"executionStats\")` Analysis\n\n```js\ndb.users.find({ email: \"farshad@test.com\" }).explain(\"executionStats\");\n```\n\n#### Critical Metrics:\n- **`stage`**: `IXSCAN` (Index scan - Good) vs `COLLSCAN` (Collection scan - Bad full table scan).\n- **`totalDocsExamined` vs `nReturned`**: In an optimal query, `totalDocsExamined == nReturned`. If `totalDocsExamined = 100,000` but `nReturned = 1`, the index is inefficient.\n- **`totalKeysExamined`**: Number of index entries scanned.\n- **`executionTimeMillis`**: Total wall-clock time.\n- **`SORT stage`**: If present without index support, MongoDB limits in-memory sort to **100MB RAM** (or throws an error unless `allowDiskUse` is enabled).', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2158, 'How do Write Concerns (`w: \"majority\"`, `j: true`) and Read Concerns (`\"majority\"`, `\"linearizable\"`) work in MongoDB Replica Sets?', '### Write Concern & Read Concern\n\n```\n+-----------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Write Concern         | Behavior                                                    |\n+-----------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| w: 1                  | Returns ACK after primary writes to memory (Fast, risk loss)|\n| w: \"majority\"         | Returns ACK only after majority of replica set nodes write  |\n| j: true               | Guarantees data is committed to on-disk Journal file        |\n+-----------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Read Concern `\"majority\"`**: Guarantees data returned has been written by a majority of nodes and cannot be rolled back during primary step-down election.\n- **Read Concern `\"linearizable\"`**: Prevents stale reads from partitioned primary nodes by contacting peers before returning.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2159, 'How does MongoDB Sharding distribute data across shards and what are the trade-offs of Hashed vs Range-Based Shard Keys?', '### Sharding Topologies & Shard Key Selection\n\n```\n[Client] ---> [Mongos Routers] ---> [Config Database (CSRS)]\n                     |\n        +------------+------------+\n        |                         |\n    [Shard 1]                 [Shard 2]\n(Replica Set)             (Replica Set)\n```\n\n- **Range-Based Shard Key**: Groups adjacent ranges of data on the same shard. Excellent for range queries (`$gte`, `$lte`), but risks hot-spotting if monotonically increasing (e.g. `createdAt`).\n- **Hashed Shard Key**: Uses MD5 hash of key to distribute writes uniformly across all shards. Eliminates write bottlenecks, but converts range queries into broadcast queries across all shards.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2160, 'How do Change Streams (`watch()`) enable real-time reactive architectures without polling in MongoDB?', '### MongoDB Change Streams\n\nChange Streams utilize the internal replication Oplog (`local.oplog.rs`) to stream real-time DML notifications with low latency.\n\n```js\nconst collection = db.collection(\'orders\');\nconst changeStream = collection.watch([\n    { $match: { \'fullDocument.status\': \'paid\' } }\n], { fullDocument: \'updateLookup\' });\n\nchangeStream.on(\'change\', (next) => {\n    console.log(\'Order status updated in real-time:\', next.fullDocument);\n    triggerWebhook(next.fullDocument);\n});\n```', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2161, 'How do TTL (Time-To-Live) Indexes automatically expire and delete ephemeral documents in MongoDB?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\ndb.sessions.createIndex({ createdAt: 1 }, { expireAfterSeconds: 3600 }) automatically removes documents after 1 hour via background thread.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2162, 'What are Partial Indexes and how do they save RAM and disk compared to Sparse Indexes?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nPartial indexes use a filter expression (e.g. { partialFilterExpression: { rating: { $gt: 4 } } }) to index only relevant subset of documents.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2163, 'How do Multi-Document ACID Transactions operate with client sessions in MongoDB 4.0+?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nUses session.startTransaction(), performs CRUD across collections, and commits atomically with session.commitTransaction().\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2164, 'What is the Bucket Pattern in MongoDB and how does it optimize high-frequency Time-Series data / IoT telemetry?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nAggregates minute/hour sensor readings into a single document containing array of measurements, avoiding collection bloat.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2165, 'How does Schema Validation with $jsonSchema enforce type and field constraints on collections?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nConfigured on createCollection with validator: { $jsonSchema: { required: [\'email\'], properties: { email: { bsonType: \'string\' } } } }.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2166, 'How does the $facet stage in the Aggregation Framework compute multi-bucket analytics in a single database pass?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nExecutes multiple sub-pipelines simultaneously (e.g. calculating price ranges, manufacturer counts, and top ratings in 1 query).\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2167, 'What are Geospatial 2dsphere indexes and how do $nearSphere and $geoWithin query coordinates?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nIndexes Earth spherical geometry (GeoJSON Point, Polygon) to calculate distances and radius queries in meters.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2168, 'How does the WiredTiger Storage Engine allocate memory for its internal cache vs OS filesystem cache?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nAllocates 50% of (RAM - 1GB) to WiredTiger cache for uncompressed pages; remaining RAM used by OS filesystem cache for compressed disk I/O.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2169, 'What is an Arbiter in MongoDB Replica Sets and why is a dedicated data-bearing secondary preferred in modern architecture?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nAn arbiter participates in voting during elections without holding data; can cause split-brain edge cases if network partitions occur.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2170, 'How does the Outlier Pattern handle skewed data distributions (e.g. a celebrity with 10 million followers)?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nStores standard users with embedded arrays and flags outliers with hasOverflow: true, storing overflow followers in a separate collection.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2171, 'What is the difference between $set, $unset, $inc, and $mul update operators in MongoDB?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\n$set updates fields, $unset removes fields, $inc increments numeric counters atomically, $mul multiplies values.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2172, 'How does Read Preference (primaryPreferred, secondaryPreferred, nearest) affect query routing and replication lag?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nDirects reads to secondaries for reporting but exposes application to stale data due to asynchronous replication lag.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2173, 'What is a Targeted Query vs a Broadcast (Scatter-Gather) Query in a sharded MongoDB cluster?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nTargeted queries include the Shard Key in WHERE clause (routed to 1 shard); Broadcast queries lack Shard Key and query all shards.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2174, 'How does Text Indexing with $text and $meta textScore calculate search relevance rankings in MongoDB?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nCreates an inverted stem token index and scores documents based on keyword frequency and weight multipliers.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2175, 'How does the Subset Pattern improve performance for pages that only display recent reviews or top comments?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nEmbeds the 5 most recent reviews in the main product document and loads remaining historical reviews on demand from a secondary collection.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
