(2255, 'How does Schema Validation with $jsonSchema enforce type and field constraints on collections?', '### MongoDB Document Database Engineering\n\nConfigured on createCollection with validator: { $jsonSchema: { required: [\'email\'], properties: { email: { bsonType: \'string\' } } } }.\n\n#### Core Architectural Principle:\nDesign schemas based on your application\'s data access patterns, and always verify index coverage with `explain(\'executionStats\')`.', 'MongoDB Document Database from Zero to Advanced'),
(2256, 'How does the WiredTiger storage engine manage memory between its internal cache and the OS page cache, and how does cache eviction operate?', '### WiredTiger Cache & Eviction Architecture\n\n```bash\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         Total Server RAM                     |\n|  +--------------------------------+-----------------------+  |\n|  | WiredTiger Cache (50% RAM - 1G)| OS Page Cache (RAM)   |  |\n|  | - Uncompressed Pages           | - Compressed Disk I/O |  |\n|  | - Active Working Set           | - File System Blocks  |  |\n|  +--------------------------------+-----------------------+  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Eviction Triggers**: When dirty pages exceed `eviction_dirty_target` (default 5%) or total cache fills past `eviction_target` (default 80%), dedicated background eviction threads compress and write pages to disk.\n- **Application Thread Eviction**: If cache fills to 95%, client worker threads are forced to perform storage eviction, causing dramatic application latency spikes.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2257, 'What are WiredTiger Read and Write Tickets, and what causes ticket exhaustion during traffic spikes?', '### WiredTiger Read/Write Tickets\n\nWiredTiger restricts concurrent storage engine operations using a ticket queue (default: **128 concurrent read tickets** and **128 concurrent write tickets**).\n\n```\nIncoming Operations ---> [Ticket Queue (128 Read / 128 Write)] ---> WiredTiger Engine\n```\n\n- **Ticket Exhaustion**: When long-running unindexed queries (`COLLSCAN`) hold tickets, all available 128 tickets are consumed. Subsequent client requests are blocked in a wait queue, causing connection pool exhaustion and application timeouts.\n- **Diagnosis**: Inspect `db.serverStatus().wiredTiger.concurrentTransactions`.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2258, 'How do Chunk Migrations work in a Sharded MongoDB cluster and how does the Balancer avoid locking traffic?', '### Sharded Chunk Migration Lifecycle\n\n1. **Initialization**: The Balancer on the primary config server identifies an unbalanced chunk distribution and issues a `moveChunk` command to the donor shard.\n2. **Cloning Phase**: The recipient shard copies all documents in the chunk range from the donor shard via network stream.\n3. **Catch-Up Phase**: The recipient shard applies any live DML modifications that occurred on the donor shard during the cloning phase.\n4. **Critical Section (Brief Lock)**: The donor shard pauses writes to that specific chunk for milliseconds, transfers final metadata, and updates the Config Server routing table.\n5. **Cleanup**: The donor shard asynchronously deletes old chunk documents via garbage collection.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2259, 'What causes Jumbo Chunks in MongoDB Sharding and how do you resolve them without downtime?', '### Jumbo Chunks & Remediation\n\n- **Cause**: A chunk exceeds the maximum chunk size (default: 64MB) and cannot be split because all documents in the chunk share the exact same shard key value (**Low Cardinality / High Frequency Key**).\n- **Impact**: The Balancer cannot move Jumbo chunks, creating permanent data skew and disk hot-spots.\n\n#### Remediation Strategy:\n1. Find chunk bounds with `sh.status(true)`.\n2. Use `sh.splitAt(\"database.collection\", { shardKey: value })` if distinct values exist.\n3. In modern MongoDB (5.0+), refine the shard key by adding a suffix field to increase cardinality: `sh.refineCollectionShardKey(\'db.coll\', { currentKey: 1, extraField: 1 })`.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2260, 'How does Zone Sharding (Tag-Aware Sharding) enforce geographic data residency and hardware tiering in MongoDB?', '### Zone Sharding (Tag-Aware Sharding)\n\nZone Sharding associates specific shard key ranges with designated shard nodes.\n\n```js\n// 1. Tag Shards based on geography:\nsh.addShardTag(\"shard_eu_01\", \"EU_ZONE\");\nsh.addShardTag(\"shard_us_01\", \"US_ZONE\");\n\n// 2. Assign Shard Key ranges to Zones (e.g. for GDPR compliance):\nsh.addTagRange(\"shop.users\", { country: \"DE\", _id: MinKey }, { country: \"DE\", _id: MaxKey }, \"EU_ZONE\");\nsh.addTagRange(\"shop.users\", { country: \"US\", _id: MinKey }, { country: \"US\", _id: MaxKey }, \"US_ZONE\");\n```\n*The Balancer automatically routes and maintains European user records exclusively on EU shards.*', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2261, 'How does the MongoDB Replication Oplog operate, and how do you calculate the Oplog Window to prevent replication failure?', '### Oplog (`local.oplog.rs`) Mechanics\n\nThe Oplog is an internal, idempotent circular capped collection containing the stream of all data mutations on the primary node.\n\n#### Oplog Window Calculation:\n```js\n// Inspect Oplog buffer capacity:\nrs.printReplicationInfo();\n```\n- **Oplog Window**: The duration in hours/days that the Oplog can retain change history before overwriting oldest entries.\n- **Risk**: If a secondary goes offline longer than the Oplog Window, its replication state falls off the tail of the Oplog (`Oplog Falloff`), requiring a full initial resync (`resync`) from scratch.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2262, 'How does MongoDB handle Rollbacks during ungraceful primary failovers, and how do you inspect rollback BSON files?', '### Rollback Mechanics & BSON Recovery\n\nIf a primary node accepts writes with `w: 1` and crashes before replicating them to secondaries, the newly elected primary will not contain those writes.\n\n1. When the old primary rejoins as a secondary, it discovers its divergence.\n2. It reverts uncommitted writes and dumps the rolled-back documents to disk as `.bson` files in the `rollback/` directory.\n3. **Recovery**: Inspect and replay documents using `bsondump` or `mongorestore`:\n```bash\nbsondump data/rollback/shop.orders.2026-08-19.bson\nmongorestore --collection orders --db shop data/rollback/shop.orders.2026-08-19.bson\n```', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2263, 'How do Multi-Document Distributed Transactions execute across multiple shards using Two-Phase Commit (2PC)?', '### Distributed Transactions Across Shards\n\n```\n[Client Session] ---> [Router / Mongos (Transaction Coordinator)]\n                               |\n           +-------------------+-------------------+\n           | (Prepare / Commit)| (Prepare / Commit)\n           v                   v\n       [Shard 1]           [Shard 2]\n```\n\n1. **Phase 1 (Prepare)**: The coordinator instructs participating shards to acquire locks and write transaction records to internal rollback/commit tables.\n2. **Phase 2 (Commit)**: If all shards successfully prepare, the coordinator writes a commit decision to the config servers and commands all shards to finalize writes atomically.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2264, 'How does the Query Optimizer Plan Cache work and how do you create Index Filters using `planCacheSetFilter`?', '### Plan Cache & Index Filters\n\nThe Query Optimizer creates candidate plans for new query shapes, runs them in parallel for a short trial period, and saves the fastest plan in the **Plan Cache**.\n\n- **Cache Eviction**: Plan cache entries are evicted when indexes are created/dropped, or collection thresholds change.\n- **Index Filters (`planCacheSetFilter`)**: Forces the optimizer to evaluate only specific indexes for a given query shape, bypassing optimizer ambiguity without changing application code.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2265, 'How do Window Functions in the Aggregation Framework (`$setWindowFields`) compute moving averages and dense rankings?', '### `$setWindowFields` Window Operations in MongoDB\n\n```js\ndb.stock_prices.aggregate([\n    { $setWindowFields: {\n        partitionBy: \"$ticker\",\n        sortBy: { date: 1 },\n        output: {\n            // 7-day Exponential Moving Average:\n            movingAvgPrice: {\n                $avg: \"$price\",\n                window: {\n                    range: [-7, \"current\"],\n                    unit: \"day\"\n                }\n            },\n            // Daily Rank:\n            rank: {\n                $denseRank: {}\n            }\n        }\n    } }\n]);\n```', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2266, 'What are the compound Multikey Index restrictions regarding parallel array indexing in MongoDB?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nA compound index cannot index more than one array field (e.g. indexing { tags: 1, categories: 1 } fails if both are arrays) to prevent Cartesian index expansion.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2267, 'How do Wildcard Indexes ($**) dynamically index arbitrary polymorphic JSON attribute key-value pairs?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\ndb.products.createIndex({ \'customAttributes.$**\': 1 }) indexes all nested dynamic fields within the customAttributes subdocument.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2268, 'How does Causal Consistency in client sessions guarantee read-your-writes across distributed replica sets?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nClient sessions attach cluster logical timestamps to requests so secondaries wait until their local replication catches up to that timestamp before reading.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2269, 'What is the difference between Write Concern w: 1, w: majority, and w: 0 in high-throughput data ingest?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nw:0 sends without ack; w:1 acks on primary memory; w:majority waits for replication consensus across quorum nodes.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2270, 'How does the Database Profiler level 2 capture slow queries, lock times, and execution plans in system.profile?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\ndb.setProfilingLevel(2, { slowms: 50 }) logs all queries taking >50ms to system.profile capped collection.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2271, 'What is the 100MB RAM limit on aggregation pipeline stages and how does allowDiskUse: true bypass it?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nMemory-intensive stages ($sort, $group) fail if they exceed 100MB RAM; setting allowDiskUse: true spills intermediate data to temporary disk files.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2272, 'How do Hidden and Delayed Replica Set members provide automated disaster recovery against accidental drops?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nDelayed members replicate with a fixed lag (e.g. 4 hours behind), allowing DBAs to copy data before dropped collections propagate.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2273, 'What are Resharding operations in MongoDB 5.0+ and how does the cluster change shard keys with zero downtime?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nCoordinates shadow collection creation, dual writing, background data sync, and an atomic namespace swap.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2274, 'How does Change Stream Resume Token (_data) ensure zero message loss during consumer service restarts?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nPassing resumeAfter: token to collection.watch() replays missed change stream events starting from that exact Oplog offset.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2275, 'What is the Outlier Pattern and how does it prevent BSON document size bloat for viral social entities?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nStores standard entities inline and offloads overflow relational elements into an overflow collection with boolean flags.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2276, 'How does the WiredTiger Checkpoint mechanism flush dirty pages without locking concurrent writes?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nPerforms copy-on-write snapshotting of dirty pages to disk every 60 seconds without blocking active in-memory reads/writes.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2277, 'What is Read Concern snapshot and how does it ensure point-in-time multi-document isolation in transactions?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nProvides a consistent snapshot view of data across multiple collections, immune to phantom updates from concurrent transactions.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2278, 'How to diagnose and mitigate lock contention and ticket starvation in high-concurrency MongoDB clusters?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nAnalyze db.serverStatus().wiredTiger.concurrentTransactions and eliminate COLLSCAN table scans that monopolize tickets.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2279, 'How does Full-Text Index stemming, stop words, and language tokenization operate in MongoDB text indexes?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nTokenizes words into root stems and removes common stop words, weighting matches by field relevance.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2280, 'How to execute Point-In-Time Recovery (PITR) by replaying incremental Oplog entries up to an exact timestamp?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nRestores baseline storage snapshot and replays local.oplog.rs entries up to the target timestamp using mongorestore --oplogReplay.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2281, 'What are the compound Multikey Index restrictions regarding parallel array indexing in MongoDB?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nA compound index cannot index more than one array field (e.g. indexing { tags: 1, categories: 1 } fails if both are arrays) to prevent Cartesian index expansion.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
