(2283, 'How does Causal Consistency in client sessions guarantee read-your-writes across distributed replica sets?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nClient sessions attach cluster logical timestamps to requests so secondaries wait until their local replication catches up to that timestamp before reading.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2284, 'What is the difference between Write Concern w: 1, w: majority, and w: 0 in high-throughput data ingest?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nw:0 sends without ack; w:1 acks on primary memory; w:majority waits for replication consensus across quorum nodes.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2285, 'How does the Database Profiler level 2 capture slow queries, lock times, and execution plans in system.profile?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\ndb.setProfilingLevel(2, { slowms: 50 }) logs all queries taking >50ms to system.profile capped collection.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2286, 'What is the 100MB RAM limit on aggregation pipeline stages and how does allowDiskUse: true bypass it?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nMemory-intensive stages ($sort, $group) fail if they exceed 100MB RAM; setting allowDiskUse: true spills intermediate data to temporary disk files.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2287, 'How do Hidden and Delayed Replica Set members provide automated disaster recovery against accidental drops?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nDelayed members replicate with a fixed lag (e.g. 4 hours behind), allowing DBAs to copy data before dropped collections propagate.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2288, 'What are Resharding operations in MongoDB 5.0+ and how does the cluster change shard keys with zero downtime?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nCoordinates shadow collection creation, dual writing, background data sync, and an atomic namespace swap.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2289, 'How does Change Stream Resume Token (_data) ensure zero message loss during consumer service restarts?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nPassing resumeAfter: token to collection.watch() replays missed change stream events starting from that exact Oplog offset.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2290, 'What is the Outlier Pattern and how does it prevent BSON document size bloat for viral social entities?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nStores standard entities inline and offloads overflow relational elements into an overflow collection with boolean flags.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2291, 'How does the WiredTiger Checkpoint mechanism flush dirty pages without locking concurrent writes?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nPerforms copy-on-write snapshotting of dirty pages to disk every 60 seconds without blocking active in-memory reads/writes.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2292, 'What is Read Concern snapshot and how does it ensure point-in-time multi-document isolation in transactions?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nProvides a consistent snapshot view of data across multiple collections, immune to phantom updates from concurrent transactions.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2293, 'How to diagnose and mitigate lock contention and ticket starvation in high-concurrency MongoDB clusters?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nAnalyze db.serverStatus().wiredTiger.concurrentTransactions and eliminate COLLSCAN table scans that monopolize tickets.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2294, 'How does Full-Text Index stemming, stop words, and language tokenization operate in MongoDB text indexes?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nTokenizes words into root stems and removes common stop words, weighting matches by field relevance.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2295, 'How to execute Point-In-Time Recovery (PITR) by replaying incremental Oplog entries up to an exact timestamp?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nRestores baseline storage snapshot and replays local.oplog.rs entries up to the target timestamp using mongorestore --oplogReplay.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2296, 'What are the compound Multikey Index restrictions regarding parallel array indexing in MongoDB?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nA compound index cannot index more than one array field (e.g. indexing { tags: 1, categories: 1 } fails if both are arrays) to prevent Cartesian index expansion.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2297, 'How do Wildcard Indexes ($**) dynamically index arbitrary polymorphic JSON attribute key-value pairs?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\ndb.products.createIndex({ \'customAttributes.$**\': 1 }) indexes all nested dynamic fields within the customAttributes subdocument.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
