(2353, 'How to diagnose and mitigate lock contention and ticket starvation in high-concurrency MongoDB clusters?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nAnalyze db.serverStatus().wiredTiger.concurrentTransactions and eliminate COLLSCAN table scans that monopolize tickets.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2354, 'How does Full-Text Index stemming, stop words, and language tokenization operate in MongoDB text indexes?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nTokenizes words into root stems and removes common stop words, weighting matches by field relevance.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2355, 'How to execute Point-In-Time Recovery (PITR) by replaying incremental Oplog entries up to an exact timestamp?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nRestores baseline storage snapshot and replays local.oplog.rs entries up to the target timestamp using mongorestore --oplogReplay.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2356, 'What are the compound Multikey Index restrictions regarding parallel array indexing in MongoDB?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nA compound index cannot index more than one array field (e.g. indexing { tags: 1, categories: 1 } fails if both are arrays) to prevent Cartesian index expansion.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2357, 'How do Wildcard Indexes ($**) dynamically index arbitrary polymorphic JSON attribute key-value pairs?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\ndb.products.createIndex({ \'customAttributes.$**\': 1 }) indexes all nested dynamic fields within the customAttributes subdocument.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2358, 'How does Causal Consistency in client sessions guarantee read-your-writes across distributed replica sets?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nClient sessions attach cluster logical timestamps to requests so secondaries wait until their local replication catches up to that timestamp before reading.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2359, 'What is the difference between Write Concern w: 1, w: majority, and w: 0 in high-throughput data ingest?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\nw:0 sends without ack; w:1 acks on primary memory; w:majority waits for replication consensus across quorum nodes.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2360, 'How does the Database Profiler level 2 capture slow queries, lock times, and execution plans in system.profile?', '### Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture\n\ndb.setProfilingLevel(2, { slowms: 50 }) logs all queries taking >50ms to system.profile capped collection.\n\n#### Expert Architecture Guideline:\nBalance shard key cardinality and monotonicity, monitor WiredTiger ticket queue latency, and ensure read/write concerns match your consistency requirements.', 'Advanced MongoDB & Distributed Architecture'),
(2361, 'What are the key differences between PhpRedis and Predis drivers in Laravel, and why is PhpRedis recommended for production?', '### PhpRedis vs Predis in Laravel\n\n```\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Feature               | PhpRedis (C Extension)      | Predis (Pure PHP)           |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Performance           | High speed (Compiled C code)| Slower (Userland PHP parsing)|\n| Memory Overhead       | Low                         | Higher per connection       |\n| Installation          | Requires PHP PECL extension | composer require predis/predis|\n| Persistent Connections| Native support              | Emulated                    |\n| Serialization         | Built-in (igbinary, msgpack)| PHP native serialize()      |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Recommendation**: Always use **`PhpRedis`** (`\'client\' => env(\'REDIS_CLIENT\', \'phpredis\')` in `config/database.php`) in production environments for 3x-6x lower CPU overhead and native connection pooling.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2362, 'How do Redis Data Types (Strings, Hashes, Lists, Sets, Sorted Sets, HyperLogLogs) map to real-world Laravel features?', '### Redis Data Structures & Laravel Use Cases\n\n```\n+--------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| Redis Data Type    | Core Commands                    | Laravel Architectural Feature     |\n+--------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| Strings            | SET, GET, INCRBY, EXPIRE         | Cache::remember, Rate Limiting    |\n| Hashes             | HSET, HGET, HGETALL, HINCRBY     | User Sessions, Shopping Carts     |\n| Lists              | LPUSH, RPOP, BRPOP, LTRIM        | Background Job Queues             |\n| Sets               | SADD, SREM, SMEMBERS, SISMEMBER  | Unique Visitor IDs, Active Users  |\n| Sorted Sets (ZSet) | ZADD, ZRANGEBYSCORE, ZREVRANK    | Delayed Jobs, Gaming Leaderboards |\n| HyperLogLog        | PFADD, PFCOUNT, PFMERGE          | Cardinality (Millions of Unique IP|\n|                    |                                  | counting with fixed 12KB RAM)     |\n| Streams            | XADD, XREADGROUP, XACK           | Event Sourcing, Audit Logging     |\n+--------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n```', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2363, 'How does Laravel Horizon monitor Redis queues, and what are the Auto and Simple balancing strategies?', '### Laravel Horizon Queue Architecture\n\nLaravel Horizon provides a real-time dashboard and code-driven supervisor configuration for Redis-backed queues.\n\n```\nIncoming Jobs ---> [Redis Queue (ZSet / List)] ---> [Horizon Worker Supervisor]\n                                                            |\n                                          +-----------------+-----------------+\n                                          | (Auto-Scaled)   | (Auto-Scaled)   |\n                                      [Worker 1]        [Worker 2]        [Worker N]\n```\n\n#### Balancing Strategies (`config/horizon.php`):\n- **`auto`**: Dynamically redistributes worker processes across queues based on real-time throughput and queue wait time (e.g. shifts workers from `low` to `high` queue when `high` backs up).\n- **`simple`**: Splits the configured worker pool evenly across all queues without dynamic reallocation.\n- **`false`**: No balancing; processes queues in the exact static order specified.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2364, 'How does Laravel prevent Cache Stampedes (Thundering Herd) using Atomic Locks and `Cache::lock()`?', '### Cache Stampede Prevention & Atomic Locks\n\nWhen a popular cache key expires, thousands of concurrent requests hit the database simultaneously to regenerate the cached value (**Cache Stampede**).\n\n#### Solution: Redis Atomic Distributed Lock\n```php\n$val = Cache::get(\'trending_products\');\n\nif ($val === null) {\n    // Acquire 10-second atomic lock, wait up to 5 seconds if another worker is computing:\n    $lock = Cache::lock(\'trending_products_lock\', 10);\n    \n    try {\n        $lock->block(5, function () use (&$val) {\n            // Double-check cache in case previous worker already populated it:\n            $val = Cache::get(\'trending_products\');\n            if ($val === null) {\n                $val = Product::where(\'is_trending\', true)->get();\n                Cache::put(\'trending_products\', $val, now()->addMinutes(30));\n            }\n        });\n    } finally {\n        $lock->release();\n    }\n}\n```', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2365, 'How do Redis Eviction Policies (`allkeys-lru`, `volatile-lru`, `allkeys-lfu`, `noeviction`) affect Laravel Cache vs Queue instances?', '### Redis Memory Eviction Policies\n\nConfigured via `maxmemory-policy` in `redis.conf`:\n\n```\n+---------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Policy              | Eviction Behavior                                             |\n+---------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| allkeys-lru         | Evicts least recently used keys out of ALL keys (Best for Cache)|\n| volatile-lru        | Evicts LRU keys ONLY among keys with an expiration TTL set    |\n| allkeys-lfu         | Evicts least frequently used keys (tracks access frequency)   |\n| noeviction          | Returns OOM error on writes when memory full (MUST use for Queue)|\n+---------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n> **Critical Architecture Rule**: **Never share a single Redis instance for both Cache and Queue with `allkeys-lru`!** If memory fills up, Redis will evict queue jobs, silently deleting pending background tasks! Use separate Redis instances/databases.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2366, 'How does Laravel implement Delayed Jobs and Priority Queues using Redis Lists and Sorted Sets under the hood?', '### Redis Queue Internal Mechanics\n\n1. **Immediate Jobs**: Pushed to a Redis List using `LPUSH queues:default` and popped by workers using blocking `BRPOP`.\n2. **Delayed Jobs (`delay(now()->addMinutes(10))`**: Stored in a Redis Sorted Set (`ZSet`) named `queues:default:delayed` where the score is the **Unix timestamp** when the job should execute.\n3. **Queue Worker Polling**: Workers run `ZRANGEBYSCORE queues:default:delayed -inf [current_timestamp]` to pop ready jobs and move them to the immediate processing List.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2367, 'How do you implement Rate Limiting and Job Throttling in Laravel using `Redis::throttle()` and `Redis::funnel()`?', '### Redis Job Throttling & Rate Limiting\n\n```php\nclass ProcessThirdPartyApiJob implements ShouldQueue {\n    use Dispatchable, InteractsWithQueue, Queueable, SerializesModels;\n\n    public function handle(): void {\n        // Allow up to 10 requests every 60 seconds with a 5-second lock timeout:\n        Redis::throttle(\'third_party_api\')\n            ->allow(10)\n            ->every(60)\n            ->block(5)\n            ->then(function () {\n                // Perform API call\n                Http::post(\'https://api.external.com/pay\');\n            }, function () {\n                // Could not acquire lock, release job back to queue with 10s delay:\n                $this->release(10);\n            });\n    }\n}\n```', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2368, 'How do Unique Jobs (`ShouldBeUnique` and `ShouldBeUniqueUntilProcessing`) prevent duplicate job dispatching in Laravel?', '### Unique Jobs in Laravel\n\n```php\nuse Illuminate\\Contracts\\Queue\\ShouldBeUnique;\n\nclass GenerateMonthlyInvoiceJob implements ShouldQueue, ShouldBeUnique {\n    public int $uniqueFor = 3600; // Lock held for 1 hour\n\n    public function __construct(public int $userId) {}\n\n    // Unique key identifier stored in Redis:\n    public function uniqueId(): string {\n        return (string) $this->userId;\n    }\n}\n```\n- **`ShouldBeUnique`**: Disallows dispatching another job with same `uniqueId` until the current job finishes executing.\n- **`ShouldBeUniqueUntilProcessing`**: Releases the unique lock as soon as a worker **starts processing** the job.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2369, 'How do you execute atomic multi-step operations using Lua Scripts (`Redis::eval()`) in Laravel?', '### Lua Scripting in Laravel\n\nLua scripts execute atomically on the Redis server, guaranteeing no other operation runs in between.\n\n```php\n// Atomic rate limiter and credit decrement:\n$script = <<<\'LUA\'\n    local balance = redis.call(\'get\', KEYS[1])\n    local cost = tonumber(ARGV[1])\n    if balance and tonumber(balance) >= cost then\n        redis.call(\'decrby\', KEYS[1], cost)\n        return 1\n    else\n        return 0\n    end\nLUA;\n\n$success = Redis::eval($script, 1, \'user:101:credits\', 50);\nif ($success) {\n    echo \'Credits deducted successfully!\';\n}\n```', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2370, 'What are the architectural differences between Redis Sentinel (High Availability) and Redis Cluster (Horizontal Sharding)?', '### Redis Sentinel vs Redis Cluster\n\n```\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Feature               | Redis Sentinel              | Redis Cluster               |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Primary Goal          | High Availability (HA)      | Horizontal Scalability (Sharding)|\n| Sharding / Slicing    | No (All data on Master node)| Yes (16,384 Hash Slots)     |\n| Failover Automation   | Sentinel monitors & promotes| Cluster nodes elect masters |\n| Multi-Key Operations  | Supported (Single instance) | Restricted to same Hash Tag |\n| Best Fit in Laravel   | Moderate dataset, 99.99% HA | Multi-terabyte datasets     |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\nIn Laravel, configure Sentinel under `database.redis.options.replication = \'sentinel\'` with master service name and sentinel host lists.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2371, 'How do Cache Tags (Cache::tags([\'users\', \'reports\'])) work internally in Redis and what is the invalidation overhead?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nLaravel creates tag namespaces in Redis using UUIDs; invalidating a tag increments the tag version key, instantly making all child cached keys unreachable.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2372, 'What are the differences between RDB (Snapshotting) and AOF (Append-Only File) persistence in Redis?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nRDB takes periodic point-in-time binary dumps (fast recovery, small disk); AOF logs every write command (zero data loss, larger files).\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2373, 'How does Redis Pub/Sub integrate with Laravel Event Broadcasting and Laravel Reverb / WebSockets?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nBroadcast events publish to Redis channels; WebSockets servers (Reverb/Soketi) subscribe to channels and push messages to frontend browsers.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2374, 'How do Redis Streams (XADD, XREADGROUP) differ from standard Redis Pub/Sub in event-driven microservices?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nPub/sub is fire-and-forget (lost if subscriber offline); Streams persist event logs on disk and support Consumer Groups and message acknowledgments (XACK).\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2375, 'How to configure multi-tenant cache separation using dynamic Redis key prefixes in Laravel?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nSet \'prefix\' => \'tenant_\' . tenant(\'id\') . \':\' in config/database.php or use Redis::connection(\'tenant\')->setPrefix().\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2376, 'How does Redis HyperLogLog (PFADD, PFCOUNT) count 100 million unique IP addresses with only 12KB RAM?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nUses probabilistic cardinality estimation with fixed 12KB memory and a standard error rate of 0.81%.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2377, 'How to handle Redis connection timeouts, retries, and read/write timeouts in config/database.php?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nConfigure \'read_timeout\' => 2.5, \'timeout\' => 2.0, and \'retry_interval\' => 100 in the Redis connection options.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2378, 'How does the Redlock algorithm implement fault-tolerant distributed locking across multi-node Redis clusters?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nAcquires locks across N independent master nodes (quorum of N/2 + 1) within a strict time limit to prevent split-brain locks.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2379, 'How to use Redis Bitmaps (SETBIT, GETBIT, BITCOUNT) to track daily active user retention in Laravel?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nStores 1 bit per user ID (1 = active, 0 = inactive); 1 million daily active users consume only ~122KB RAM.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2380, 'What is the difference between Redis pipeline() and transaction() (MULTI/EXEC) in Laravel?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\npipeline() sends batch commands asynchronously to reduce network round-trips; transaction() ensures atomic isolated execution with MULTI/EXEC.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2381, 'How do you configure Redis Geospatial indexes (GEOADD, GEODIST, GEORADIUS) in Laravel?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nStores longitude/latitude in Sorted Sets using 52-bit geohashes for fast radius and proximity calculations.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2382, 'How to prevent Redis memory fragmentation and optimize the memory allocator (Jemalloc)?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nMonitor memory fragmentation ratio (used_memory_rss / used_memory); trigger active defragmentation with activedefrag yes in redis.conf.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2383, 'How does the XFetch (Probabilistic Early Expiration) algorithm prevent cache stampedes without explicit locks?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nRecomputes and refreshes cached items probabilistically before the actual TTL expires, smoothing out database load.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2384, 'How do you configure Redis as a high-performance session handler in Laravel (SESSION_DRIVER=redis)?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nStores serialized user session payloads in Redis with automated TTL expiration, decoupling session storage from web servers.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2385, 'How to safely flush or clear specific tagged keys in Redis without calling FLUSHDB in production?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nNever call FLUSHDB in production; use SCAN with MATCH pattern and UNLINK (asynchronous non-blocking deletion).\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(2386, 'How do Cache Tags (Cache::tags([\'users\', \'reports\'])) work internally in Redis and what is the invalidation overhead?', '### Redis & Laravel Architecture\n\nLaravel creates tag namespaces in Redis using UUIDs; invalidating a tag increments the tag version key, instantly making all child cached keys unreachable.\n\n#### Architectural Rule:\nIsolate Cache and Queue Redis instances, enforce connection timeouts, and use atomic distributed locks to prevent race conditions.', 'Redis & Laravel Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
