(2566, 'What are WebSocket Close Codes (1000 Normal, 1001 Going Away, 1006 Abnormal, 1008 Policy Violation)?', '### High-Performance C++ WebSockets\n\nStandard status codes sent inside Close frames to explain disconnection reasons according to RFC 6455.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Rule:\nDisable Nagle\'s algorithm (`TCP_NODELAY`), implement backpressure high-watermark checks, and use zero-copy binary serialization formats (FlatBuffers/SBE).', 'High-Performance WebSockets in C++'),
(2567, 'How does SSL/TLS (WSS) integration with OpenSSL operate in C++ WebSocket servers?', '### High-Performance C++ WebSockets\n\nWraps standard TCP stream in an SSL_CTX / SSL socket, performing TLS 1.3 handshake and encrypting frame bytes before socket transmission.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Rule:\nDisable Nagle\'s algorithm (`TCP_NODELAY`), implement backpressure high-watermark checks, and use zero-copy binary serialization formats (FlatBuffers/SBE).', 'High-Performance WebSockets in C++'),
(2568, 'What is the difference between Text Frames (0x1) and Binary Frames (0x2) in WebSocket protocols?', '### High-Performance C++ WebSockets\n\nText frames must be strictly validated as valid UTF-8 sequences (invalid UTF-8 triggers 1007 Close); Binary frames contain raw unvalidated bytes.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Rule:\nDisable Nagle\'s algorithm (`TCP_NODELAY`), implement backpressure high-watermark checks, and use zero-copy binary serialization formats (FlatBuffers/SBE).', 'High-Performance WebSockets in C++'),
(2569, 'How do Continuation Frames (0x0) handle large streamed messages across multiple WebSocket chunks?', '### High-Performance C++ WebSockets\n\nFirst frame has FIN=0 and opcode (text/binary); intermediate frames have FIN=0 and opcode 0x0; final frame has FIN=1 and opcode 0x0.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Rule:\nDisable Nagle\'s algorithm (`TCP_NODELAY`), implement backpressure high-watermark checks, and use zero-copy binary serialization formats (FlatBuffers/SBE).', 'High-Performance WebSockets in C++'),
(2570, 'How does lock-free single-producer single-consumer (SPSC) ring buffer architecture optimize multi-threaded WebSocket broadcasting?', '### High-Performance C++ WebSockets\n\nEnables worker threads to push outbound messages to network threads with zero mutex contention and sub-50 nanosecond latency.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Rule:\nDisable Nagle\'s algorithm (`TCP_NODELAY`), implement backpressure high-watermark checks, and use zero-copy binary serialization formats (FlatBuffers/SBE).', 'High-Performance WebSockets in C++'),
(2571, 'How does io_uring with IORING_SETUP_SQPOLL eliminate system calls in ultra-low latency C++ WebSocket engines?', '### io_uring SQPOLL Architecture\n\nTraditional `epoll` requires multiple user-to-kernel context switches (`epoll_wait`, `read`, `write`).\n\n- **`IORING_SETUP_SQPOLL`**: Creates a dedicated kernel polling thread that continuously inspects the Submission Queue (SQ) ring buffer shared directly via `mmap()`.\n- **Zero-Syscall Operation**: The C++ application submits read/write operations by writing directly to shared memory without issuing `enter()` or `syscall` instructions, achieving sub-microsecond frame dispatch.\n\n```cpp\nstruct io_uring_params params = {};\nparams.flags = IORING_SETUP_SQPOLL | IORING_SETUP_SQ_AFF;\nparams.sq_thread_cpu = 3; // Pin kernel polling thread to dedicated core\nio_uring_queue_init_params(4096, &ring, &params);\n```', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2572, 'How do Kernel TLS (kTLS) and hardware crypto offload eliminate userspace OpenSSL encryption overhead in C++ WebSockets?', '### Kernel TLS (kTLS) & Hardware Offloading\n\nStandard userspace OpenSSL requires copying encrypted data between kernel and userspace memory buffers (`read()` -> decrypt in userspace -> re-encrypt -> `write()`).\n\n```\nUserspace Buffer ---> [kTLS Socket (TCP_ULP)] ---> [Kernel / SmartNIC Hardware AES-GCM Offload]\n```\n\n1. Complete TLS 1.3 handshake in userspace (OpenSSL/BoringSSL).\n2. Extract symmetric session keys and transfer ownership to kernel socket via `setsockopt(fd, SOL_TCP, TCP_ULP, \"tls\", 4)`.\n3. Send raw WebSocket frame bytes with `sendfile()` or `vmsplice()` directly from disk or memory buffers without userspace encryption overhead.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2573, 'How do you accelerate WebSocket payload unmasking and UTF-8 validation using AVX2 and AVX-512 SIMD vectorization in C++?', '### AVX2 / AVX-512 SIMD Vectorization\n\nUnmasking a 64KB WebSocket frame sequentially byte-by-byte takes thousands of CPU cycles. AVX2 processes **32 bytes per instruction**, and AVX-512 processes **64 bytes per instruction**.\n\n```cpp\n#include <immintrin.h>\n\nvoid unmaskAVX2(char* payload, size_t length, const uint8_t mask[4]) {\n    // Replicate 4-byte mask across 256-bit register (32 bytes):\n    __m256i mask_vec = _mm256_set1_epi32(*reinterpret_cast<const int32_t*>(mask));\n    \n    size_t i = 0;\n    for (; i + 32 <= length; i += 32) {\n        __m256i data = _mm256_loadu_si256(reinterpret_cast<const __m256i*>(payload + i));\n        __m256i unmasked = _mm256_xor_si256(data, mask_vec);\n        _mm256_storeu_si256(reinterpret_cast<__m256i*>(payload + i), unmasked);\n    }\n    // Handle tail remainder:\n    for (; i < length; ++i) payload[i] ^= mask[i % 4];\n}\n```', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2574, 'How do Lock-Free Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) Ring Buffers achieve zero-contention inter-thread WebSocket communication?', '### Lock-Free SPSC Ring Buffer with Cache Alignment\n\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T, size_t Capacity>\nclass SpscRingBuffer {\n    static_assert((Capacity & (Capacity - 1)) == 0, \"Capacity must be a power of 2\");\n    T buffer_[Capacity];\n    \n    // alignas(64) prevents False Sharing between producer and consumer CPU cache lines:\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> head_{0};\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> tail_{0};\npublic:\n    bool push(const T& item) {\n        size_t current_tail = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n        if (current_tail - head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire) == Capacity) return false; // Full\n        buffer_[current_tail & (Capacity - 1)] = item;\n        tail_.store(current_tail + 1, std::memory_order_release);\n        return true;\n    }\n    bool pop(T& item) {\n        size_t current_head = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n        if (current_head == tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // Empty\n        item = buffer_[current_head & (Capacity - 1)];\n        head_.store(current_head + 1, std::memory_order_release);\n        return true;\n    }\n};\n```', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2575, 'How do you configure NUMA Node Pinning and CPU core isolation for deterministic sub-microsecond WebSocket processing?', '### NUMA Node Pinning & CPU Core Isolation\n\nOn multi-socket servers, accessing memory attached to a remote NUMA node increases latency by 2x–3x (**Remote Memory Access Penalty**).\n\n#### Low-Latency Setup:\n1. **Isolate CPU Cores**: Add `isolcpus=2-7 nohz_full=2-7 rcu_nocbs=2-7` to Linux kernel boot parameters.\n2. **Pin Memory to Local NUMA Node**: Allocate memory buffers locally via `numa_alloc_onnode()`.\n3. **Bind Thread Affinity**: Bind WebSocket worker threads strictly to the isolated cores on the same NUMA node.\n```cpp\n#include <pthread.h>\n\nvoid pinThreadToCore(int core_id) {\n    cpu_set_t cpuset;\n    CPU_ZERO(&cpuset);\n    CPU_SET(core_id, &cpuset);\n    pthread_setaffinity_np(pthread_self(), sizeof(cpu_set_t), &cpuset);\n}\n```', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2576, 'How do custom Polymorphic Memory Resources (`std::pmr`) and Monotonic Buffer Arenas eliminate heap fragmentation in continuous WebSocket streaming?', '### `std::pmr` Monotonic Buffer Arenas\n\nCalling `malloc()` / `free()` repeatedly for thousands of incoming WebSocket frames causes heap lock contention and memory fragmentation.\n\n```cpp\n#include <memory_resource>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid handleFrameStream(const char* data, size_t len) {\n    // 64KB stack-backed buffer arena (Zero heap allocation!):\n    char stack_buf[65536];\n    std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource pool(stack_buf, sizeof(stack_buf));\n    \n    std::pmr::vector<OrderDelta> deltas(&pool);\n    deltas.reserve(100);\n    \n    parseDeltas(data, len, deltas);\n    processDeltas(deltas);\n    // Pool resets instantly when out of scope with zero destructor/free calls\n}\n```', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2577, 'How do you design a High-Frequency Trading (HFT) Market Data Dissemination Engine over WebSockets with Simple Binary Encoding (SBE)?', '### HFT Market Data Dissemination with SBE\n\n**Simple Binary Encoding (SBE)** uses direct struct casting with fixed offsets, outperforming Protobuf and JSON by orders of magnitude.\n\n```cpp\n#pragma pack(push, 1)\nstruct MarketUpdateHeader {\n    uint16_t blockLength;\n    uint16_t templateId;\n    uint16_t schemaId;\n    uint16_t version;\n};\nstruct OrderBookLevel {\n    uint64_t price;\n    uint32_t quantity;\n    uint8_t  side; // 0 = Buy, 1 = Sell\n};\n#pragma pack(pop)\n\nvoid broadcastUpdate(uWS::WebSocket<false, true, UserData>* ws, const OrderBookLevel& lvl) {\n    // Directly casts binary struct to buffer and sends frame immediately:\n    ws->send(std::string_view(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&lvl), sizeof(OrderBookLevel)), uWS::OpCode::BINARY);\n}\n```', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2578, 'How do you detect and mitigate Slowloris WebSocket frame attacks and fragmentation starvation at line rate in C++?', '### Mitigating WebSocket Starvation Attacks\n\nAn attacker sends incomplete continuation frames (`FIN=0`) 1 byte per second, forcing the server to hold connection state in memory indefinitely.\n\n#### Defense Architecture:\n1. **Fragmentation Window Timeout**: If a multi-frame message is not completed within a fixed deadline (e.g. 500ms), terminate the socket (`1008 Policy Violation`).\n2. **Max Accumulated Message Buffer**: Enforce hard cap (e.g. 1MB); abort immediately if accumulated frame buffers exceed limit.\n3. **Minimum Byte Transfer Rate**: Track `bytes_received / delta_time`; disconnect sockets with transfer rate < 1KB/sec.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2579, 'How does eBPF / XDP (eXpress Data Path) inspect and filter malicious WebSocket upgrade traffic before reaching userspace sockets?', '### eBPF / XDP Kernel Packet Filtering\n\nXDP executes BPF bytecode directly in the network card driver before allocating Linux kernel `sk_buff` memory.\n\n```c\n// XDP eBPF filter inspecting TCP SYN and HTTP Upgrade packets:\nSEC(\"xdp\")\nint xdp_websocket_filter(struct xdp_md *ctx) {\n    void *data = (void *)(long)ctx->data;\n    void *data_end = (void *)(long)ctx->data_end;\n    \n    // Parse Ethernet -> IP -> TCP headers\n    // If source IP is in blocklist or exceeds rate limit threshold:\n    if (is_rate_limited(src_ip)) {\n        return XDP_DROP; // Drops packet in hardware at 100M packets/sec!\n    }\n    return XDP_PASS;\n}\n```', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2580, 'How do you implement Dynamic Multi-Tiered Backpressure with Adaptive Frame Dropping in C++ WebSocket clusters?', '### Multi-Tiered Backpressure Heuristics\n\n```\nProducer Stream ---> [Tier 1: Low Watermark (256KB)] ---> Normal Delivery\n                     [Tier 2: High Watermark (1MB)]   ---> Drop Non-Critical Delta Frames\n                     [Tier 3: Max Buffer (4MB)]       ---> Force Disconnect (1008 / Slow Consumer)\n```\n\nIn financial tickers, if a consumer falls behind, the engine drops intermediate price updates and delivers only the latest consolidated snapshot, preserving real-time accuracy without exhausting server memory.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2581, 'How does TCP Small Queues (TSQ) and BQL (Byte Queue Limits) eliminate bufferbloat in high-throughput C++ WebSocket servers?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nLimits amount of unacknowledged data buffered inside kernel queues, keeping socket RTT and delivery jitter minimal.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2582, 'What are the architectural trade-offs of Kernel-Bypass networking (Solarflare OpenOnload / DPDK) for C++ WebSockets?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nBypasses Linux TCP/IP stack to read packets directly from NIC memory, reducing latency to <500ns at the cost of dedicated CPU polling.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2583, 'How does TLS 1.3 0-RTT Early Data work over WebSockets and how do you protect against Replay Attacks?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nClient sends encrypted data in initial TLS handshake packet; server rejects non-idempotent early requests to prevent replay attacks.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2584, 'What are the memory layout benefits of Structure of Arrays (SoA) vs Array of Structures (AoS) in WebSocket connection state managers?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nSoA groups same-attribute arrays contiguously in RAM, maximizing CPU cache line efficiency during broadcast loops.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2585, 'How do you manage sequence numbers and gap detection for L3 Full Order Book delta replication over WebSockets?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nAttach monotonically increasing sequence ID to every frame; client requests snapshot resync if missing sequence numbers are detected.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `question`, `answer`, `category`) VALUES
(2586, 'How does memory fence ordering (acquire/release vs sequential consistency) impact atomic session publication in multithreaded servers?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nAcquire-release enforces memory visibility boundaries across cores without issuing expensive full bus-lock instructions (MFENCE).\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2587, 'What is Zero-Copy TCP streaming with vmsplice() and tee() syscalls in Linux C++ servers?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nMoves memory pages directly between user memory and pipe buffers without copying data bytes through the CPU.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2588, 'How do you configure SO_INCOMING_CPU to route WebSocket socket events to the CPU core handling NIC interrupts?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nAligns kernel softirq processing with user-space worker thread to achieve perfect CPU cache warmth for TCP packets.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2589, 'How does fastutf8 validation achieve 4GB/sec validation throughput for incoming WebSocket text frames?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nUses SIMD lookup tables to validate UTF-8 byte sequences in parallel chunks rather than state machine branches.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2590, 'What is the difference between Aeron IPC shared memory transport and WebSockets for local intra-host messaging?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nAeron IPC uses memory-mapped files and lock-free rings for sub-100ns IPC; WebSockets bridge external network clients.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2591, 'How to design a resilient distributed WebSocket fan-out cluster using NATS JetStream and Kafka backplanes in C++?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nWorker nodes consume partitioned Kafka streams and fan out binary frames to millions of connected WebSocket clients.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2592, 'How to detect memory leaks and trace allocator performance under 1M concurrent WebSocket connections using Jemalloc profiling?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nEnable MALLOC_CONF=prof:true to generate heap allocation dumps and identify memory fragmentation hot-spots.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2593, 'What are the mechanics of RFC 6455 RSV bits (RSV1, RSV2, RSV3) for custom protocol extensions in C++?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nRSV bits signal custom frame compression, end-to-end encryption, or proprietary multiplexing layers.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2594, 'How does epoll edge-triggered (EPOLLET) mode differ from level-triggered mode when draining large WebSocket frame queues?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nEdge-triggered notifies only on state transitions; workers must loop read()/write() until EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK is returned.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2595, 'How to benchmark and measure tail latency (p99, p99.9, p99.99) jitter in C++ WebSocket servers under synthetic load?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nUse HDRHistogram to measure high-percentile latency distributions without falling victim to the Coordinated Omission problem.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2596, 'How does TCP Small Queues (TSQ) and BQL (Byte Queue Limits) eliminate bufferbloat in high-throughput C++ WebSocket servers?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nLimits amount of unacknowledged data buffered inside kernel queues, keeping socket RTT and delivery jitter minimal.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
(2597, 'What are the architectural trade-offs of Kernel-Bypass networking (Solarflare OpenOnload / DPDK) for C++ WebSockets?', '### Advanced C++ WebSocket & Low-Latency Architecture\n\nBypasses Linux TCP/IP stack to read packets directly from NIC memory, reducing latency to <500ns at the cost of dedicated CPU polling.\n\n#### Low-Latency Engineering Standard:\nUtilize `io_uring` with SQPOLL, pin threads to isolated NUMA cores, and enforce SIMD vectorization for frame parsing.', 'Advanced C++ WebSocket Internals & Low-Latency Systems'),
