(3096, 'What are the internal phases of the Node.js Libuv Event Loop and in what exact order do they execute?', '### Libuv Event Loop Phases\n\n```\n   ┌───────────────────────────┐\n┌─>│          timers           │  (setTimeout, setInterval)\n│  └─────────────┬─────────────┘\n│  ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐\n│  │     pending callbacks     │  (I/O errors, deferred system callbacks)\n│  └─────────────┬─────────────┘\n│  ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐\n│  │       idle, prepare       │  (Internal Libuv use only)\n│  └─────────────┬─────────────┘\n│  ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐\n│  │           poll            │  (Retrieves new I/O events; blocks if idle)\n│  └─────────────┬─────────────┘\n│  ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐\n│  │           check           │  (setImmediate callbacks)\n│  └─────────────┬─────────────┘\n│  ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐\n└──┤      close callbacks      │  (socket.on(\'close\', ...))\n   └───────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n- **Microtasks Priority**: `process.nextTick()` and `Promise.then()` run **immediately after the current operation finishes**, before the Event Loop proceeds to the next phase.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3097, 'What is the exact execution difference between `process.nextTick()`, `Promise.then()`, `setImmediate()`, and `setTimeout(fn, 0)`?', '### Microtasks vs Macrotasks Execution Order\n\n```\n1. Synchronous Code Execution (Call Stack)\n2. process.nextTick Queue (Highest priority microtask)\n3. Other Microtasks (Promise.then, queueMicrotask)\n4. Event Loop Phase Macrotasks (Timers -> Poll -> Check: setImmediate)\n```\n\n#### Code Example:\n```js\nconsole.log(\'1. Sync\');\n\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\'5. setTimeout\'), 0);\nsetImmediate(() => console.log(\'6. setImmediate\'));\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\'3. Promise.then\'));\nprocess.nextTick(() => console.log(\'2. nextTick\'));\n\nconsole.log(\'4. Sync End\');\n\n// Output: 1. Sync -> 4. Sync End -> 2. nextTick -> 3. Promise.then -> 5. setTimeout -> 6. setImmediate\n```', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3098, 'How does the Libuv Thread Pool work in Node.js, and which core modules utilize it instead of non-blocking OS I/O?', '### Libuv Thread Pool & `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE`\n\nWhile network sockets (TCP/HTTP) use non-blocking kernel mechanisms (`epoll`/`kqueue`/`IOCP`) on the main thread, certain operations cannot be performed asynchronously by the OS and run on the **Libuv Thread Pool** (default: **4 worker threads**).\n\n```\nMain Event Loop ---> [Libuv Thread Pool (UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=4)] ---> OS Disk / CPU\n```\n\n#### Modules using Libuv Thread Pool:\n1. **`fs` (File System)**: All file read/write/stat operations.\n2. **`crypto`**: Password hashing (`crypto.pbkdf2`, `crypto.scrypt`, `crypto.randomBytes`).\n3. **`zlib`**: Compression and decompression (`zlib.gzip`, `zlib.deflate`).\n4. **`dns.lookup`**: Synchronous DNS resolution via `getaddrinfo`.\n\n*Tune with: `process.env.UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE = 16;` before any async call.*', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3099, 'How do Node.js Streams (Readable, Writable, Duplex, Transform) handle Backpressure using `pipeline()` and `highWaterMark`?', '### Streams & Backpressure Management\n\nWhen a fast reader produces data faster than a slow writer can consume, memory usage explodes.\n\n```\nFast Readable ---> [highWaterMark Buffer (Default: 16KB)] ---> Slow Writable (Returns false)\n       ^                                                                |\n       +------------------- (Emits \'drain\' event) <---------------------+\n```\n\n#### Safe Stream Piping with `stream.pipeline`:\n```js\nconst fs = require(\'fs\');\nconst zlib = require(\'zlib\');\nconst { pipeline } = require(\'stream/promises\');\n\nasync function compressLargeFile(source, destination) {\n    // Automatically handles backpressure, cleans up file descriptors, and handles errors:\n    await pipeline(\n        fs.createReadStream(source),\n        zlib.createGzip(),\n        fs.createWriteStream(destination)\n    );\n    console.log(\'Compression complete with zero memory bloat.\');\n}\n```', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3100, 'What are the memory and security differences between `Buffer.alloc()`, `Buffer.allocUnsafe()`, and `Buffer.from()`?', '### Node.js Buffer Memory Allocation\n\n```\n+----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Method               | Memory Initialization       | Security / Performance      |\n+----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Buffer.alloc(size)   | Fills memory with zeroes    | Safe (No data leaks, slower)|\n| Buffer.allocUnsafe() | Allocates uninitialized RAM | Fast (Risks leaking old RAM)|\n| Buffer.from(data)    | Copies existing array/string| Safe copy                   |\n+----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\n> **Security Danger with `Buffer.allocUnsafe()`**: The allocated memory contains raw un-zeroed RAM chunks previously used by other processes, which may leak passwords, TLS private keys, or API tokens if sent over the network!', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3101, 'How do Worker Threads (`worker_threads`) differ from Child Processes (`child_process`), and how do you share memory with `SharedArrayBuffer`?', '### Worker Threads vs Child Processes\n\n```\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Feature               | Worker Threads              | Child Processes (fork/spawn)|\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Process Type          | Same OS process, new thread | Completely separate process |\n| Memory Model          | Shared memory (SharedArrayBuffer)| Isolated memory (IPC copy) |\n| Startup Overhead      | Low (~1-2ms)                | High (~30-50ms)             |\n| Communication         | MessagePort / Shared Memory | IPC Pipes / stdio           |\n| Best Use Case         | CPU-intensive algorithms    | Running external binaries   |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\n#### Atomic Shared Memory with `Atomics`:\n```js\nconst sharedBuffer = new SharedArrayBuffer(4);\nconst uint32 = new Uint32Array(sharedBuffer);\nAtomics.add(uint32, 0, 10); // Atomic lock-free increment across threads\n```', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3102, 'How does the Cluster Module scale Node.js across multiple CPU cores, and how does the Master process distribute TCP sockets?', '### Node.js Cluster Module\n\nBy default, Node.js runs on a single CPU core. The Cluster module spawns multiple worker processes sharing the same server port.\n\n```\n[Master Process (Primary)] ---> Listens on Port 8080\n             |\n    +--------+--------+ (Distributes incoming TCP connections via Round-Robin)\n    v                 v\n[Worker 1]        [Worker 2]        [Worker N (1 per CPU core)]\n```\n\n```js\nconst cluster = require(\'cluster\');\nconst http = require(\'http\');\nconst numCPUs = require(\'os\').cpus().length;\n\nif (cluster.isPrimary) {\n    for (let i = 0; i < numCPUs; i++) cluster.fork();\n    cluster.on(\'exit\', (worker) => cluster.fork()); // Auto-restart on crash\n} else {\n    http.createServer((req, res) => res.end(\'Handled by worker \' + process.pid)).listen(8080);\n}\n```', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3103, 'How do you implement a Graceful Shutdown procedure in Node.js to close HTTP servers and database connection pools cleanly?', '### Graceful Shutdown in Node.js\n\n```js\nconst server = app.listen(3000);\n\nfunction shutdown(signal) {\n    console.log(`Received ${signal}. Shutting down gracefully...`);\n    \n    // 1. Stop accepting new incoming HTTP connections:\n    server.close(async () => {\n        console.log(\'Closed all active HTTP connections.\');\n        try {\n            // 2. Close database pools & Redis clients:\n            await db.pool.end();\n            await redis.quit();\n            console.log(\'Database connections closed cleanly.\');\n            process.exit(0);\n        } catch (err) {\n            console.error(\'Error during shutdown:\', err);\n            process.exit(1);\n        }\n    });\n\n    // 3. Force shutdown if graceful steps stall past 10 seconds:\n    setTimeout(() => {\n        console.error(\'Shutdown deadline exceeded. Forcing termination.\');\n        process.exit(1);\n    }, 10000).unref();\n}\n\nprocess.on(\'SIGTERM\', () => shutdown(\'SIGTERM\'));\nprocess.on(\'SIGINT\', () => shutdown(\'SIGINT\'));\n```', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3104, 'How is the V8 Garbage Collection Heap structured (New Space / Scavenge vs Old Space / Mark-Sweep) in Node.js?', '### V8 Garbage Collection Memory Spaces\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                V8 Engine Heap                               |\n|  +-------------------------------+---------------------------------------+  |\n|  | New Space (Semi-Spaces)       | Old Space                             |  |\n|  | - From-Space & To-Space       | - Old Pointer Space                   |  |\n|  | - Scavenge GC (Fast, frequent)| - Old Data Space                      |  |\n|  | - Short-lived objects         | - Mark-Sweep & Mark-Compact GC (Heavy)|  |\n|  +-------------------------------+---------------------------------------+  |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n1. **Scavenge GC (Young Generation)**: Fast allocation. Surviving objects are promoted to Old Space after 2 GC cycles.\n2. **Mark-Sweep-Compact (Old Generation)**: Traverses roots, marks live objects, sweeps unreachable memory, and defragments pages.\n3. **Tuning**: Increase heap capacity with `--max-old-space-size=4096` (4GB).', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3105, 'How do you prevent Prototype Pollution and Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) vulnerabilities in Node.js?', '### Prototype Pollution & ReDoS Defenses\n\n- **Prototype Pollution**: Occurs when unvalidated user JSON modifies `Object.prototype` (e.g. `__proto__.isAdmin = true`).\n  - **Defense**: Use `Object.create(null)` for map dictionaries, or freeze prototypes with `Object.freeze(Object.prototype)`.\n\n- **ReDoS (Catastrophic Backtracking)**: Evil regexes with overlapping quantifiers (e.g. `/(a+)+$/`) take hours of CPU for non-matching strings, freezing the single-threaded Event Loop.\n  - **Defense**: Use non-backtracking engines (e.g. Google\'s `re2`) or enforce timeouts with `validator.js`.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3106, 'What are the differences between CommonJS (require) and ES Modules (import) in Node.js runtime?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nCommonJS is synchronous and evaluates at runtime; ESM is asynchronous, supports top-level await, and performs static tree-shaking.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3107, 'How does the EventLoopUtilization (ELU) metric in perf_hooks measure true Node.js event loop lag?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nCalculates the ratio of active time spent executing JavaScript vs idle time waiting for I/O in the poll phase.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3108, 'How do you debug memory leaks in Node.js using Chrome DevTools and heap snapshot comparison?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nConnect via node --inspect, capture 3 heap snapshots during load, and filter by Objects Allocated Between Snapshot 1 and 2.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3109, 'What is the difference between spawn(), fork(), and exec() in the child_process module?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nspawn streams raw stdio buffers; exec buffers output into a string with maxBuffer limit; fork creates a new V8 instance with built-in IPC channel.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3110, 'How does crypto.timingSafeEqual() prevent timing attacks when comparing cryptographic signatures and password hashes?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nExecutes in constant time regardless of where string characters differ, preventing side-channel timing analysis.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3111, 'What are AsyncLocalStorage and AsyncLocalStorage.run() in async_hooks and how do they track request context?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nProvides thread-local storage equivalents in async Node.js to propagate correlation IDs across asynchronous call chains.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3112, 'How does HTTP/2 multiplexing work in Node.js http2 module compared to HTTP/1.1 connection pooling?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nStreams multiple concurrent requests and responses over a single persistent TCP connection with binary framing.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3113, 'What is the difference between uncaughtException and unhandledRejection event handlers in Node.js process?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nuncaughtException catches synchronous errors; unhandledRejection catches rejected promises without a .catch() handler.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3114, 'How do you optimize Node.js cold starts and memory usage in serverless environments (AWS Lambda)?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nBundle code into single small file with esbuild, lazy-load heavy dependencies, and reuse global database connections outside handlers.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3115, 'How does the Node.js Diagnostic Report (process.report.generateReport()) assist in analyzing crashed production servers?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nGenerates a comprehensive JSON dump containing stack traces, OS memory stats, Libuv handles, and loaded native shared libraries.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3116, 'What are the performance trade-offs of using SIMD and WebAssembly (WASM) modules in Node.js compute engines?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nExecutes near-native CPU instructions for image processing and cryptography without invoking external C++ child processes.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3117, 'How to build custom Transform streams for real-time JSON parsing and CSV line formatting?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nExtend stream.Transform and implement _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) and _flush(callback).\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `question`, `answer`, `category`) VALUES
(3118, 'How does the Node.js Permission Model (--experimental-permission) restrict file system and network access?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nEnforces runtime sandbox security flags (e.g. --allow-fs-read=/tmp) to prevent malicious npm packages from reading disk.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3119, 'How to profile CPU bottlenecks and generate interactive Flame Graphs using 0x or Clinic.js Flame?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nRuns sampling profiler over V8 code stacks to visualize which functions occupy the largest percentage of CPU time.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3120, 'How do you implement atomic file writing in Node.js to prevent corrupted partial files during power loss?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nWrite to a temporary file in the same filesystem directory and perform an atomic fs.rename() to the target filename.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3121, 'What are the differences between CommonJS (require) and ES Modules (import) in Node.js runtime?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nCommonJS is synchronous and evaluates at runtime; ESM is asynchronous, supports top-level await, and performs static tree-shaking.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3122, 'How does the EventLoopUtilization (ELU) metric in perf_hooks measure true Node.js event loop lag?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nCalculates the ratio of active time spent executing JavaScript vs idle time waiting for I/O in the poll phase.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3123, 'How do you debug memory leaks in Node.js using Chrome DevTools and heap snapshot comparison?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nConnect via node --inspect, capture 3 heap snapshots during load, and filter by Objects Allocated Between Snapshot 1 and 2.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3124, 'What is the difference between spawn(), fork(), and exec() in the child_process module?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nspawn streams raw stdio buffers; exec buffers output into a string with maxBuffer limit; fork creates a new V8 instance with built-in IPC channel.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3125, 'How does crypto.timingSafeEqual() prevent timing attacks when comparing cryptographic signatures and password hashes?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nExecutes in constant time regardless of where string characters differ, preventing side-channel timing analysis.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
