(3181, 'What are the differences between CommonJS (require) and ES Modules (import) in Node.js runtime?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nCommonJS is synchronous and evaluates at runtime; ESM is asynchronous, supports top-level await, and performs static tree-shaking.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3182, 'How does the EventLoopUtilization (ELU) metric in perf_hooks measure true Node.js event loop lag?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nCalculates the ratio of active time spent executing JavaScript vs idle time waiting for I/O in the poll phase.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3183, 'How do you debug memory leaks in Node.js using Chrome DevTools and heap snapshot comparison?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nConnect via node --inspect, capture 3 heap snapshots during load, and filter by Objects Allocated Between Snapshot 1 and 2.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3184, 'What is the difference between spawn(), fork(), and exec() in the child_process module?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nspawn streams raw stdio buffers; exec buffers output into a string with maxBuffer limit; fork creates a new V8 instance with built-in IPC channel.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3185, 'How does crypto.timingSafeEqual() prevent timing attacks when comparing cryptographic signatures and password hashes?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nExecutes in constant time regardless of where string characters differ, preventing side-channel timing analysis.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3186, 'What are AsyncLocalStorage and AsyncLocalStorage.run() in async_hooks and how do they track request context?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nProvides thread-local storage equivalents in async Node.js to propagate correlation IDs across asynchronous call chains.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3187, 'How does HTTP/2 multiplexing work in Node.js http2 module compared to HTTP/1.1 connection pooling?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nStreams multiple concurrent requests and responses over a single persistent TCP connection with binary framing.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3188, 'What is the difference between uncaughtException and unhandledRejection event handlers in Node.js process?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nuncaughtException catches synchronous errors; unhandledRejection catches rejected promises without a .catch() handler.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3189, 'How do you optimize Node.js cold starts and memory usage in serverless environments (AWS Lambda)?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nBundle code into single small file with esbuild, lazy-load heavy dependencies, and reuse global database connections outside handlers.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3190, 'How does the Node.js Diagnostic Report (process.report.generateReport()) assist in analyzing crashed production servers?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nGenerates a comprehensive JSON dump containing stack traces, OS memory stats, Libuv handles, and loaded native shared libraries.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3191, 'What are the performance trade-offs of using SIMD and WebAssembly (WASM) modules in Node.js compute engines?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nExecutes near-native CPU instructions for image processing and cryptography without invoking external C++ child processes.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3192, 'How to build custom Transform streams for real-time JSON parsing and CSV line formatting?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nExtend stream.Transform and implement _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) and _flush(callback).\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3193, 'How does the Node.js Permission Model (--experimental-permission) restrict file system and network access?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nEnforces runtime sandbox security flags (e.g. --allow-fs-read=/tmp) to prevent malicious npm packages from reading disk.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3194, 'How to profile CPU bottlenecks and generate interactive Flame Graphs using 0x or Clinic.js Flame?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nRuns sampling profiler over V8 code stacks to visualize which functions occupy the largest percentage of CPU time.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3195, 'How do you implement atomic file writing in Node.js to prevent corrupted partial files during power loss?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nWrite to a temporary file in the same filesystem directory and perform an atomic fs.rename() to the target filename.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3196, 'What are the differences between CommonJS (require) and ES Modules (import) in Node.js runtime?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nCommonJS is synchronous and evaluates at runtime; ESM is asynchronous, supports top-level await, and performs static tree-shaking.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3197, 'How does the EventLoopUtilization (ELU) metric in perf_hooks measure true Node.js event loop lag?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nCalculates the ratio of active time spent executing JavaScript vs idle time waiting for I/O in the poll phase.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3198, 'How do you debug memory leaks in Node.js using Chrome DevTools and heap snapshot comparison?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nConnect via node --inspect, capture 3 heap snapshots during load, and filter by Objects Allocated Between Snapshot 1 and 2.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3199, 'What is the difference between spawn(), fork(), and exec() in the child_process module?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nspawn streams raw stdio buffers; exec buffers output into a string with maxBuffer limit; fork creates a new V8 instance with built-in IPC channel.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3200, 'How does crypto.timingSafeEqual() prevent timing attacks when comparing cryptographic signatures and password hashes?', '### Node.js Runtime Architecture\n\nExecutes in constant time regardless of where string characters differ, preventing side-channel timing analysis.\n\n#### Node.js Production Standard:\nNever block the Event Loop with heavy sync computation, manage stream backpressure with `stream.pipeline`, and handle process shutdown signals gracefully.', 'Node.js Architecture from Zero to Advanced'),
(3201, 'What are the fundamental differences between Monolithic, Modular Monolith, and Microservices architectures, and what criteria determine when to transition?', '### Monolith vs Modular Monolith vs Microservices\n\n```\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Dimension             | Traditional Monolith        | Modular Monolith            | Microservices               |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Deployment Unit       | Single deployable artifact  | Single deployable artifact  | Independent distributed services|\n| Code Boundaries       | High risk of tight coupling | Strict in-process module API| Network boundaries (gRPC/HTTP)|\n| Database Architecture | Shared single database      | Module-isolated schemas     | Database-per-Service        |\n| Operational Complexity| Low (Single CI/CD pipeline) | Low/Moderate                | High (Service mesh, tracing)|\n| Team Scalability      | Bottleneck on large teams   | High (Clear code ownership) | Very High                   |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Transition Rule**: Start with a Modular Monolith. Only transition to Microservices when independent team deployment cadences and distinct scalability bottlenecks justify the operational network overhead.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3202, 'How do Domain-Driven Design (DDD) Bounded Contexts, Ubiquitous Language, and Context Maps prevent domain model corruption across teams?', '### Domain-Driven Design (DDD) Core\n\n- **Ubiquitous Language**: A shared, unambiguous language agreed upon by software engineers and domain experts within a specific context.\n- **Bounded Context**: An explicit linguistic and structural boundary within which a domain model applies.\n\n```\n[Sales Bounded Context]                          [Billing Bounded Context]\n(\"Customer\" = Sales Lead with credit rating)    (\"Customer\" = Invoiced legal entity with VAT ID)\n                         \\                              /\n                          [Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL)]\n```\n\n- **Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL)**: A translation adapter that translates external domain models into local domain entities, preventing legacy or third-party semantics from polluting local domain logic.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3203, 'What is the difference between Saga Choreography and Saga Orchestration for distributed transactions across microservices?', '### Saga Pattern: Choreography vs Orchestration\n\nIn microservices with Database-per-Service, ACID two-phase commits (2PC) cause distributed deadlocks. The **Saga Pattern** manages transactions as a series of local steps with **Compensating Transactions** on failure.\n\n```\nCHOREOGRAPHY (Event-Driven / Decentralized):\n[Order Service] --- OrderCreatedEvent ---> [Payment Service] --- PaymentApprovedEvent ---> [Inventory Service]\n\nORCHESTRATION (Centralized Coordinator):\n                     +---> [Payment Service] (Process Payment)\n                     |\n[Order Saga Manager] -+---> [Inventory Service] (Reserve Items)\n                     |\n                     +---> (If step fails) ---> [Compensating Transactions] (Refund/Cancel)\n```', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3204, 'How does the Outbox Pattern with Change Data Capture (CDC) guarantee dual-write consistency between databases and message brokers?', '### The Transactional Outbox Pattern\n\nWriting to a database and publishing an event to Kafka/RabbitMQ in the same handler risks inconsistent state if the network or process crashes after the DB commit.\n\n```\n[Application Service] ---> (Single Local ACID Transaction) ---> [Orders Table]\n                                                            +-> [Outbox Table]\n                                                                      |\n[Debezium / CDC Message Relay] <--- Reads Database Write-Ahead Log (WAL) -+\n              |\n              v\n       [Apache Kafka / RabbitMQ Queue]\n```\n\n1. Insert the domain entity and an `OutboxEvent` record in the **same local database transaction**.\n2. A background CDC engine (e.g. Debezium) tails the database transaction log and publishes events to Kafka with guaranteed at-least-once delivery.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3205, 'What is the Circuit Breaker Pattern and how do its Closed, Open, and Half-Open states prevent cascading system failures?', '### Circuit Breaker Pattern\n\n```\n           +------------------- (Failure Threshold Exceeded) -------------------+\n           |                                                                    |\n           v                                                                    |\n      [OPEN STATE] -- (Sleep Window Expires) --> [HALF-OPEN STATE] -- (Success) --> [CLOSED STATE]\n      (Fails fast /                               (Allows test traffic)             (Normal traffic)\n       Returns Fallback)                                |\n           ^                                            |\n           +-------------- (Test Request Fails) --------+\n```\n\n- **Closed**: Requests flow normally. Failures are counted.\n- **Open**: When failure rate exceeds threshold (e.g. 50%), circuit trips. All requests fail immediately without making network calls, protecting downstream services.\n- **Half-Open**: After a timeout, allows limited probe requests. If successful, resets to Closed; if failing, trips back to Open.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3206, 'What are the architectural differences between Clean Architecture, Hexagonal Architecture (Ports & Adapters), and Onion Architecture?', '### Clean Architecture & Inverted Dependencies\n\nAll three architectures adhere to the **Dependency Rule**: *Dependencies point inward toward the pure domain policy, never outward toward databases, UI, or frameworks.*\n\n```\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Frameworks & Drivers (Web, DB, Devices, UI, CLI)            |\n|   +-------------------------------------------------------+ |\n|   | Interface Adapters (Controllers, Gateways, Presenters)| |\n|   |   +-----------------------------------------------+   | |\n|   |   | Application Business Rules (Use Cases)        |   | |\n|   |   |   +---------------------------------------+   |   | |\n|   |   |   | Enterprise Domain Entities (Pure Core)|   |   | |\n|   |   |   +---------------------------------------+   |   | |\n|   |   +-----------------------------------------------+   | |\n|   +-------------------------------------------------------+ |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Hexagonal**: Focuses on **Ports** (domain interfaces) and **Adapters** (infrastructure implementations).\n- **Onion**: Focuses on domain models at center surrounded by domain services and application use cases.\n- **Clean Architecture**: Combines Hexagonal and Onion with explicit Use Case interactors.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `question`, `answer`, `category`) VALUES
(3207, 'What is the difference between the CAP Theorem and the PACELC Theorem in distributed system design?', '### CAP vs PACELC Theorem\n\n- **CAP Theorem**: In a distributed system with Network Partitions (**P**), you must choose between Consistency (**C**) or Availability (**A**).\n- **PACELC Theorem**: Extends CAP to describe behavior under normal conditions when **no partition** exists:\n\n```\nIf Partition (P)  ---> Choose Availability (A) OR Consistency (C)\nElse (E)          ---> Choose Latency (L)      OR Consistency (C)\n```\n\n#### Examples:\n- **MongoDB / MySQL Group Replication**: **PC/EC** (Consistent under partition; chooses Consistency over low Latency in normal mode).\n- **Amazon DynamoDB / Cassandra**: **PA/EL** (Available under partition; chooses low Latency over strict Consistency in normal mode).', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3208, 'How do the Strategy, State, and Command behavioral design patterns differ in purpose and implementation?', '### Strategy vs State vs Command\n\n```\n+--------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| Pattern            | Intent                           | Key Difference                    |\n+--------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| Strategy           | Interchangeable algorithms under | Client chooses strategy explicitly|\n|                    | a common interface               | to vary execution logic           |\n| State              | Object changes behavior when its | Object transitions internal state |\n|                    | internal state changes           | automatically without client code |\n| Command            | Encapsulates a request as a      | Supports undo/redo, queues, and   |\n|                    | standalone executable object     | deferred execution pipelines      |\n+--------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n```', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3209, 'How does CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) optimize high-traffic read/write workloads with separate data models?', '### CQRS Architecture\n\n```\nWrite Command ---> [Write Model / DDD Aggregates] ---> [Write DB (Normalized / ACID)]\n                                                              |\n                                             (Event Sourcing / CDC Stream)\n                                                              v\nRead Query   <--- [Read Model / Fast DTOs]      <--- [Read DB (Elasticsearch / Redis / Denormalized)]\n```\n\n- **Write Side**: Highly normalized, optimized for business invariants, validation, and transactional consistency.\n- **Read Side**: Highly denormalized read projections optimized for sub-millisecond query performance without joins.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3210, 'What are the trade-offs of Cache-Aside, Write-Through, Write-Behind (Write-Back), and Refresh-Ahead caching patterns?', '### Caching Design Patterns\n\n```\n+-----------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| Pattern               | Write Path                       | Consistency & Risk                |\n+-----------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| Cache-Aside           | App writes to DB, invalidates    | Lazy load on miss; small window of|\n|                       | cache key                        | stale data                        |\n| Write-Through         | App writes to cache; cache writes| High consistency; write latency is|\n|                       | synchronously to DB              | sum of both stores                |\n| Write-Behind (Back)   | App writes to cache; cache writes| Ultra-fast writes; data loss risk |\n|                       | asynchronously to DB in batches  | if cache crashes before DB sync   |\n| Refresh-Ahead         | Cache automatically reloads hot  | Eliminates read latency on hot    |\n|                       | keys before TTL expiration       | keys; needs predictable access    |\n+-----------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n```', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3211, 'What is the Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) pattern and when is it preferred over a single generic API Gateway?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nBFF creates dedicated backend adapters tailored to specific client UIs (Mobile, Web, IoT), reducing payload over-fetching.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3212, 'How does the Bulkhead pattern isolate critical failure domains in distributed software?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nPartitions thread pools, CPU, and memory so failure in one module (e.g. image rendering) cannot starve core checkout services.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3213, 'What are the differences between Idempotency Keys and Distributed Locks in preventing duplicate payment charges?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nIdempotency keys cache and return the original response for duplicate request IDs; distributed locks prevent concurrent simultaneous execution.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3214, 'How does the Event Sourcing pattern reconstruct entity state from an immutable append-only event log?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nStores state as an append-only stream of domain events (OrderPlaced, ItemAdded); current state is replayed from history.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3215, 'What is an Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL) in Domain-Driven Design and how does it protect modern services from legacy monoliths?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nTranslates legacy data structures into clean modern domain models at boundary adapters, preventing semantic contamination.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3216, 'How does the Strangler Fig pattern migrate monolithic applications to microservices incrementally?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nGradually replaces monolithic routes with microservices behind an API gateway until the legacy monolith is decommissioned.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3217, 'What is the difference between Service Mesh (Istio, Envoy) and standard API Gateways?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nAPI Gateway manages North-South external client traffic; Service Mesh manages East-West service-to-service mTLS and observability.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3218, 'How do Token Bucket and Leaky Bucket rate limiting algorithms differ in burst traffic handling?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nToken bucket allows controlled bursts up to bucket capacity; Leaky bucket smooths out traffic into a constant steady output rate.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3219, 'What is the difference between Value Objects, Entities, and Aggregates in Domain-Driven Design?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nEntities have unique identity; Value Objects are immutable and defined by attributes; Aggregates encapsulate transactional consistency.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3220, 'How does the Adapter pattern differ from the Facade pattern and Decorator pattern in GoF design?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nAdapter bridges incompatible interfaces; Facade simplifies a complex subsystem; Decorator adds behaviors without altering interfaces.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3221, 'What is the Flyweight design pattern and how does it minimize memory usage for millions of fine-grained objects?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nShares intrinsic immutable state across instances (e.g. character glyphs, tree textures) and stores extrinsic state outside.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3222, 'How does the Mediator pattern decouple multi-component communication into a centralized hub?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nEncapsulates how sets of objects interact, preventing components from referencing each other directly (e.g. ChatRoom, CQRS MediatR).\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3223, 'What is the Memento pattern and how is it used to implement rollback and undo/redo operations?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nCaptures an object\'s internal state without violating encapsulation, allowing restoration to a previous state.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3224, 'How does the Visitor pattern allow adding new operations to existing class hierarchies without modifying them?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nUses double dispatch (accept/visit) to execute new algorithms on elements of an object structure.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3225, 'What is Primitive Obsession and how do Value Objects eliminate this code smell?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nUsing raw integers/strings for domain concepts (email, money); Value Objects wrap primitives with validation and type safety.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3226, 'What is Feature Envy code smell and how does Move Method refactoring resolve it?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nWhen a method in Class A accesses data from Class B more than its own; fix by moving the method into Class B.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3227, 'What is Shotgun Surgery vs Divergent Change in code smell analysis?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nShotgun Surgery: one change forces edits across many classes; Divergent Change: one class is modified for many unrelated reasons.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3228, 'What is the Principle of Least Astonishment (POLA) in API and interface design?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nComponent behavior should match natural user/developer expectations with zero surprising side effects.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3229, 'How does Chaos Engineering (Chaos Monkey) build confidence in distributed system resilience?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nIntentionally injects simulated failures (latency, killed instances, network partitions) into production to verify self-healing.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3230, 'What is the difference between RPO (Recovery Point Objective) and RTO (Recovery Time Objective) in disaster recovery?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nRPO measures maximum tolerable data loss in time; RTO measures maximum tolerable downtime before systems must be restored.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3231, 'What is the Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) pattern and when is it preferred over a single generic API Gateway?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nBFF creates dedicated backend adapters tailored to specific client UIs (Mobile, Web, IoT), reducing payload over-fetching.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
