(3317, 'What is the difference between Service Mesh (Istio, Envoy) and standard API Gateways?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nAPI Gateway manages North-South external client traffic; Service Mesh manages East-West service-to-service mTLS and observability.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3318, 'How do Token Bucket and Leaky Bucket rate limiting algorithms differ in burst traffic handling?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nToken bucket allows controlled bursts up to bucket capacity; Leaky bucket smooths out traffic into a constant steady output rate.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3319, 'What is the difference between Value Objects, Entities, and Aggregates in Domain-Driven Design?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nEntities have unique identity; Value Objects are immutable and defined by attributes; Aggregates encapsulate transactional consistency.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3320, 'How does the Adapter pattern differ from the Facade pattern and Decorator pattern in GoF design?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nAdapter bridges incompatible interfaces; Facade simplifies a complex subsystem; Decorator adds behaviors without altering interfaces.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3321, 'What is the Flyweight design pattern and how does it minimize memory usage for millions of fine-grained objects?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nShares intrinsic immutable state across instances (e.g. character glyphs, tree textures) and stores extrinsic state outside.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3322, 'How does the Mediator pattern decouple multi-component communication into a centralized hub?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nEncapsulates how sets of objects interact, preventing components from referencing each other directly (e.g. ChatRoom, CQRS MediatR).\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3323, 'What is the Memento pattern and how is it used to implement rollback and undo/redo operations?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nCaptures an object\'s internal state without violating encapsulation, allowing restoration to a previous state.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3324, 'How does the Visitor pattern allow adding new operations to existing class hierarchies without modifying them?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nUses double dispatch (accept/visit) to execute new algorithms on elements of an object structure.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3325, 'What is Primitive Obsession and how do Value Objects eliminate this code smell?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nUsing raw integers/strings for domain concepts (email, money); Value Objects wrap primitives with validation and type safety.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3326, 'What is Feature Envy code smell and how does Move Method refactoring resolve it?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nWhen a method in Class A accesses data from Class B more than its own; fix by moving the method into Class B.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3327, 'What is Shotgun Surgery vs Divergent Change in code smell analysis?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nShotgun Surgery: one change forces edits across many classes; Divergent Change: one class is modified for many unrelated reasons.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3328, 'What is the Principle of Least Astonishment (POLA) in API and interface design?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nComponent behavior should match natural user/developer expectations with zero surprising side effects.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3329, 'How does Chaos Engineering (Chaos Monkey) build confidence in distributed system resilience?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nIntentionally injects simulated failures (latency, killed instances, network partitions) into production to verify self-healing.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3330, 'What is the difference between RPO (Recovery Point Objective) and RTO (Recovery Time Objective) in disaster recovery?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nRPO measures maximum tolerable data loss in time; RTO measures maximum tolerable downtime before systems must be restored.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3331, 'What is the Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) pattern and when is it preferred over a single generic API Gateway?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nBFF creates dedicated backend adapters tailored to specific client UIs (Mobile, Web, IoT), reducing payload over-fetching.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3332, 'How does the Bulkhead pattern isolate critical failure domains in distributed software?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nPartitions thread pools, CPU, and memory so failure in one module (e.g. image rendering) cannot starve core checkout services.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3333, 'What are the differences between Idempotency Keys and Distributed Locks in preventing duplicate payment charges?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nIdempotency keys cache and return the original response for duplicate request IDs; distributed locks prevent concurrent simultaneous execution.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3334, 'How does the Event Sourcing pattern reconstruct entity state from an immutable append-only event log?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nStores state as an append-only stream of domain events (OrderPlaced, ItemAdded); current state is replayed from history.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
