(3391, 'What is the Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) pattern and when is it preferred over a single generic API Gateway?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nBFF creates dedicated backend adapters tailored to specific client UIs (Mobile, Web, IoT), reducing payload over-fetching.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3392, 'How does the Bulkhead pattern isolate critical failure domains in distributed software?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nPartitions thread pools, CPU, and memory so failure in one module (e.g. image rendering) cannot starve core checkout services.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3393, 'What are the differences between Idempotency Keys and Distributed Locks in preventing duplicate payment charges?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nIdempotency keys cache and return the original response for duplicate request IDs; distributed locks prevent concurrent simultaneous execution.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3394, 'How does the Event Sourcing pattern reconstruct entity state from an immutable append-only event log?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nStores state as an append-only stream of domain events (OrderPlaced, ItemAdded); current state is replayed from history.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3395, 'What is an Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL) in Domain-Driven Design and how does it protect modern services from legacy monoliths?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nTranslates legacy data structures into clean modern domain models at boundary adapters, preventing semantic contamination.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3396, 'How does the Strangler Fig pattern migrate monolithic applications to microservices incrementally?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nGradually replaces monolithic routes with microservices behind an API gateway until the legacy monolith is decommissioned.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3397, 'What is the difference between Service Mesh (Istio, Envoy) and standard API Gateways?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nAPI Gateway manages North-South external client traffic; Service Mesh manages East-West service-to-service mTLS and observability.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `question`, `answer`, `category`) VALUES
(3398, 'How do Token Bucket and Leaky Bucket rate limiting algorithms differ in burst traffic handling?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nToken bucket allows controlled bursts up to bucket capacity; Leaky bucket smooths out traffic into a constant steady output rate.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3399, 'What is the difference between Value Objects, Entities, and Aggregates in Domain-Driven Design?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nEntities have unique identity; Value Objects are immutable and defined by attributes; Aggregates encapsulate transactional consistency.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3400, 'How does the Adapter pattern differ from the Facade pattern and Decorator pattern in GoF design?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nAdapter bridges incompatible interfaces; Facade simplifies a complex subsystem; Decorator adds behaviors without altering interfaces.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3401, 'What is the Flyweight design pattern and how does it minimize memory usage for millions of fine-grained objects?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nShares intrinsic immutable state across instances (e.g. character glyphs, tree textures) and stores extrinsic state outside.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3402, 'How does the Mediator pattern decouple multi-component communication into a centralized hub?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nEncapsulates how sets of objects interact, preventing components from referencing each other directly (e.g. ChatRoom, CQRS MediatR).\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3403, 'What is the Memento pattern and how is it used to implement rollback and undo/redo operations?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nCaptures an object\'s internal state without violating encapsulation, allowing restoration to a previous state.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3404, 'How does the Visitor pattern allow adding new operations to existing class hierarchies without modifying them?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nUses double dispatch (accept/visit) to execute new algorithms on elements of an object structure.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3405, 'What is Primitive Obsession and how do Value Objects eliminate this code smell?', '### Software Design & Enterprise Architecture\n\nUsing raw integers/strings for domain concepts (email, money); Value Objects wrap primitives with validation and type safety.\n\n#### Architectural Golden Rule:\nDesign boundaries that isolate change, minimize cognitive overhead, and align technical models directly with business domains.', 'Software Design, Architecture Patterns & Enterprise Systems'),
(3406, 'What are the Three Laws of Test-Driven Development (TDD) formulated by Robert C. Martin (Uncle Bob)?', '### The Three Laws of TDD\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 1. You may not write production code until you have written a failing unit  |\n|    test.                                                                    |\n| 2. You may not write more of a unit test than is sufficient to fail; and    |\n|    not compiling is failing.                                                |\n| 3. You may not write more production code than is sufficient to pass the    |\n|    currently failing unit test.                                             |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n- **The Rhythm**: Developers write tiny micro-tests (taking seconds to write), see them fail immediately (Red), write the absolute minimum code to pass (Green), and then refactor safely without breaking behavior.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3407, 'What are the fundamental differences between Chicago (Classicist / Inside-Out) and London (Mockist / Outside-In) schools of TDD?', '### Chicago (Classicist) vs London (Mockist) TDD\n\n```\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Dimension             | Chicago / Detroit (Classicist)| London (Mockist / Outside-In) |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Starting Point        | Inside-Out (Core Domain)    | Outside-In (HTTP / UI / API)|\n| Verification Strategy | State Verification (Asserts)| Behavior Verification (Mocks|\n| Use of Test Doubles   | Minimal (Only for I/O / DB) | Extensive (Mock all peers)  |\n| Refactoring Risk      | Free to refactor internals  | Mocks can couple to impl    |\n| Design Driver         | Emergent domain algorithms  | Interface discovery & wiring|\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Chicago School**: Tests real collaborating objects; verifies final state (`assertEquals($expected, $actual)`).\n- **London School**: Mocks all collaborating dependencies to discover and define interface contracts first.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3408, 'How do the five types of Test Doubles (Dummy, Stub, Spy, Mock, Fake) in Gerard Meszaros\'s taxonomy differ?', '### Gerard Meszaros Test Double Taxonomy\n\n```\n+--------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Double Type        | Definition & Primary Use Case                                 |\n+--------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Dummy              | Objects passed around but never actually used (e.g. dummy arg)|\n| Stub               | Returns hardcoded canned responses to method invocations      |\n| Spy                | A Stub that secretly records how many times it was called     |\n| Mock               | Pre-programmed with expectations; asserts call count & args   |\n| Fake               | Working implementation with shortcuts (e.g. In-Memory SQLite) |\n+--------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### Code Distinction:\n```php\n// Stub:\n$paymentGateway->method(\'charge\')->willReturn(true);\n\n// Mock (verifies behavior):\n$paymentGateway->expects($this->once())->method(\'charge\')->with(100.00);\n\n// Fake:\nclass FakeUserRepository implements UserRepositoryInterface { private array $users = []; ... }\n```', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3409, 'What is the Transformation Priority Premise (TPP) in TDD and how does it guide algorithmic progression?', '### Transformation Priority Premise (TPP)\n\nFormulated by Uncle Bob, TPP states that **simpler transformations should be chosen before more complex ones** to avoid getting stuck during TDD.\n\n```\n1. ({} -> nil)               No code -> Return null\n2. (nil -> constant)         Return hardcoded constant\n3. (constant -> variable)    Use parameter variable\n4. (unconditional -> if)     Add conditional branch\n5. (statement -> recursion)  Recursive call\n6. (if -> while)             Loop iteration\n7. (expression -> function)  Extract abstraction\n```\n*Following TPP prevents premature complexity and guides natural algorithmic generalization.*', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3410, 'What is Mutation Testing (e.g. Infection PHP, Stryker) and why is high Line Coverage often a false indicator of test suite quality?', '### Mutation Testing & Mutation Score Indicator (MSI)\n\nLine coverage only proves that a line of code was **executed**, not that the test would catch a bug if that line changed.\n\n```\nProduction Code ---> [Mutator Engine (Flips \'>\' to \'<=\', changes \'+\' to \'-\')] ---> Mutated Mutant\n                                  |\n                       (Run Existing Test Suite)\n                                  |\n           +----------------------+----------------------+\n           | (Test Fails)                                | (Test Passes)\n           v                                             v\n    [Mutant KILLED! ✅]                           [Mutant ESCAPED! ❌ (Weak Test!)]\n```\n\n- **Mutation Score (MSI)**: `(Killed Mutants / Total Mutants) * 100`.\n- If a mutant changes `if ($price > 100)` to `if ($price >= 100)` and all tests still pass, the mutant **escaped**, revealing missing boundary assertion tests.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3411, 'What are the FIRST principles of unit testing in TDD?', '### FIRST Principles of Testing\n\n- **Fast**: Tests must execute in milliseconds so developers run them hundreds of times a day.\n- **Independent / Isolated**: Tests must not depend on execution order or shared mutable global state.\n- **Repeatable**: Must produce the exact same outcome in local development, CI/CD pipelines, or production replicas without network dependencies.\n- **Self-Validating**: Tests must yield a binary pass/fail boolean verdict without manual log inspections.\n- **Timely**: Written *immediately before* the production code that makes them pass.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3412, 'What is Double-Loop TDD (Acceptance Test-Driven Development - ATDD)?', '### Double-Loop TDD (ATDD / BDD Cycle)\n\nCombines high-level customer-facing acceptance tests with fine-grained unit-level TDD cycles.\n\n```\n   +-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   | Outer Loop (Acceptance Test / Feature Level)                |\n   |  1. Write failing Acceptance Test (Red)                     |\n   |                                                             |\n   |   +-----------------------------------------------------+   |\n   |   | Inner Loop (Unit TDD / Component Level)             |   |\n   |   |   2. Write failing Unit Test (Red)                  |   |\n   |   |   3. Write minimal code to pass (Green)             |   |\n   |   |   4. Refactor Unit (Refactor)                       |   |\n   |   |   (Repeat inner loop until unit feature completes)  |   |\n   |   +-----------------------------------------------------+   |\n   |                                                             |\n   |  5. Acceptance Test Passes (Green)                          |\n   |  6. Refactor System Architecture                            |\n   +-------------------------------------------------------------+\n```', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3413, 'How do you identify and refactor the \'Overspecification / Mocking Internals\' test smell?', '### Overspecification (Fragile Tests)\n\n```php\n// ❌ Fragile Overspecified Mock Test (Tests implementation details):\n$userRepo->expects($this->once())->method(\'beginTransaction\');\n$userRepo->expects($this->once())->method(\'checkCache\')->willReturn(null);\n$userRepo->expects($this->once())->method(\'executeSql\');\n$userRepo->expects($this->once())->method(\'commitTransaction\');\n```\n\n- **Problem**: If you refactor the internal caching strategy or remove transactions, the test fails even though business behavior is 100% correct.\n- **Refactoring**: **Test behavior, not implementation.** Assert that the user is persisted and queryable, rather than asserting which internal helper methods were invoked.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3414, 'How does Property-Based Testing (QuickCheck, fast-check, Hypothesis) complement Example-Based TDD?', '### Property-Based Testing vs Example-Based Testing\n\n- **Example-Based Testing**: Tests fixed developer-selected inputs (`assert(sort([3, 1, 2]) == [1, 2, 3])`).\n- **Property-Based Testing**: Defines invariant mathematical properties that must hold true for **thousands of randomly generated inputs**:\n\n```ts\n// Property: Sorting an array preserves length and elements are monotonically non-decreasing\nfc.assert(\n    fc.property(fc.array(fc.integer()), (arr) => {\n        const sorted = customSort(arr);\n        return sorted.length === arr.length &&\n               sorted.every((val, i) => i === 0 || sorted[i - 1] <= val);\n    })\n);\n```\n*Automatically shrinks complex failing edge cases (e.g. empty arrays, MAX_INT, Unicode surrogate pairs) to the smallest reproducible example.*', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3415, 'How do Consumer-Driven Contract Tests with Pact verify microservice API contracts in TDD?', '### Consumer-Driven Contract Testing with Pact\n\nInstead of spinning up 50 microservices for slow, fragile E2E integration tests:\n\n```\n[Consumer Service (TDD Mock)] ---> Generates Contract (Pact JSON File) ---> [Pact Broker Registry]\n                                                                                    |\n[Provider Service CI Pipeline] <--- Replays Pact JSON against real Provider <-------+\n```\n\n1. **Consumer writes TDD tests** against a Pact mock server defining expected requests/responses.\n2. Pact generates an immutable JSON contract file.\n3. The **Provider CI pipeline** verifies that its live endpoints satisfy all consumer contracts before deploying to production.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3416, 'What is the AAA (Arrange, Act, Assert) testing pattern and how does it compare to Given-When-Then?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nArrange sets up dependencies and state; Act executes the behavior under test; Assert verifies results and state transitions.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3417, 'What are Flaky Tests and how do you diagnose non-deterministic test failures in CI pipelines?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nTests that pass and fail intermittently due to race conditions, shared database state, or timezone/clock dependencies.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3418, 'How does Testcontainers eliminate differences between production databases and test environments?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nSpawns ephemeral real Docker containers (PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka) directly from test setup code and destroys them after tests.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3419, 'What is Assertion Roulette test smell and how do descriptive assertion messages fix it?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nHaving multiple unexplained assertions in one test where failure doesn\'t indicate which specific condition broke.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3420, 'How do you test time-dependent logic in TDD using Clock abstractions (PSR-20 ClockInterface / Carbon::setTestNow)?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nInjects a mockable clock interface rather than calling time() or new DateTime() directly in production code.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3421, 'What is the difference between Branch Coverage, Condition Coverage, and Path Coverage in code analysis?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nBranch checks all if/else paths; Condition checks all boolean sub-expressions; Path checks all possible execution combinations.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3422, 'How do you test Domain Events and Event-Driven architecture in TDD without hitting real message brokers?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nMock or spy the event dispatcher to assert that specific DomainEvent instances were published with correct payload properties.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3423, 'What is the Mystery Guest test smell and how does inline test data builders resolve it?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nWhen a test relies on hidden external fixture files, making it impossible to understand the test without reading outside files.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3424, 'How does Database Transaction Rollback (RefreshDatabase) speed up database integration tests?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nWraps each test in a database transaction and rolls back on teardown, avoiding expensive table truncations or migrations.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3425, 'What are Parameterized / Data Provider tests and how do they eliminate test method duplication?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nExecutes the same test logic against a matrix of input datasets (@dataProvider in PHPUnit / test.each in Jest).\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3426, 'How do you test asynchronous Promises and EventEmitters in Jest / Vitest without timing race conditions?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nUse async/await with resolve/reject matchers and mock timers (jest.useFakeTimers) to advance time deterministically.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3427, 'What is Characterization Testing (Approval Testing) and how does it enable safe legacy code refactoring?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nCaptures existing legacy output snapshots (Golden Master) to ensure refactoring does not alter baseline behavior.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3428, 'How do you test private methods in TDD without violating encapsulation or using reflection hacks?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nNever test private methods directly; test them through the public API methods that invoke them.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3429, 'What is the Ice Cream Cone Anti-Pattern in automated software testing?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nHaving thousands of slow, brittle UI/E2E tests and very few fast unit tests, resulting in slow and flaky CI feedback.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3430, 'How do you implement Mock Service Worker (MSW) in TypeScript for network-level API mocking?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nIntercepts HTTP requests at network layer using Service Workers, providing realistic API mocking for unit and integration tests.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
(3431, 'What is the AAA (Arrange, Act, Assert) testing pattern and how does it compare to Given-When-Then?', '### Test-Driven Development (TDD) Mastery\n\nArrange sets up dependencies and state; Act executes the behavior under test; Assert verifies results and state transitions.\n\n#### TDD Core Rule:\nWrite tests before code, verify behavior rather than implementation details, and use mutation testing to guarantee test rigor.', 'Test-Driven Development (TDD) from Zero to Advanced'),
